1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,154
Picture a hospital.

2
00:00:01,154 --> 00:00:06,923
Not just any hospital — one that has made a public promise to every patient

3
00:00:06,923 --> 00:00:10,000
inside it: the lights will never go out.

4
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:11,538
Not during a storm.

5
00:00:11,538 --> 00:00:13,846
Not during a power grid failure.

6
00:00:13,846 --> 00:00:19,231
Not even if a transformer a block away explodes at three in the morning.

7
00:00:19,231 --> 00:00:21,923
That promise isn't kept by hoping hard.

8
00:00:21,923 --> 00:00:27,692
It's kept by architecture: two separate generators, in two separate rooms, fed by two separate

9
00:00:27,692 --> 00:00:33,462
fuel lines, so that no single failure — no matter how bad — can plunge

10
00:00:33,462 --> 00:00:37,692
the building into darkness at the exact moment someone is mid-surgery.

11
00:00:37,692 --> 00:00:43,462
That hospital's promise is, structurally, identical to the promise your SOC 2 report makes about

12
00:00:43,462 --> 00:00:44,162
availability.

13
00:00:44,162 --> 00:00:49,931
And the surgery happening inside your system, right now, might be someone checking a brokerage

14
00:00:49,931 --> 00:00:55,700
balance before making a trade, or a compliance officer pulling a report five minutes before

15
00:00:55,700 --> 00:00:56,854
a filing deadline.

16
00:00:56,854 --> 00:01:01,085
To them, "the system was briefly down" isn't a shrug-worthy inconvenience.

17
00:01:01,085 --> 00:01:06,469
It's the moment your product failed them at the exact moment they needed it.

18
00:01:06,469 --> 00:01:08,008
Welcome to Phase 6.

19
00:01:08,008 --> 00:01:13,777
Over the next hour and fifty minutes, we're going to build the electrical architecture of

20
00:01:13,777 --> 00:01:19,546
that hospital, layer by layer, for a real production system — and by the end,

21
00:01:19,546 --> 00:01:25,315
you'll understand not just how to configure Multi-AZ databases and autoscaling, but why each layer

22
00:01:25,315 --> 00:01:31,085
exists, which specific failure it defends against, and — just as importantly — where the

23
00:01:31,085 --> 00:01:33,777
honest limits of each defense actually are.

24
00:01:33,777 --> 00:01:38,008
This entire phase maps to one SOC 2 control: CC7.1, availability.

25
00:01:38,008 --> 00:01:43,777
And here's what makes CC7.1 genuinely different from most of the controls you've built in

26
00:01:43,777 --> 00:01:45,700
earlier phases of this course.

27
00:01:45,700 --> 00:01:51,469
Access control and audit logging are things you can largely configure once and verify are

28
00:01:51,469 --> 00:01:53,008
still correctly configured later.

29
00:01:53,008 --> 00:01:58,777
Availability is different — it's not a static configuration, it's a claim about behavior under

30
00:01:58,777 --> 00:02:04,546
stress, and the only honest way to back up a claim about behavior under stress

31
00:02:04,546 --> 00:02:08,008
is to actually apply stress and watch what happens.

32
00:02:08,008 --> 00:02:11,085
You cannot screenshot your way to proving availability.

33
00:02:11,085 --> 00:02:15,315
You have to break things, on purpose, and film the recovery.

34
00:02:15,315 --> 00:02:21,085
Let's set up the mental model that's going to organize everything in this phase, because

35
00:02:21,085 --> 00:02:26,085
there's a genuinely useful hierarchy hiding underneath what looks like eight unrelated topics.

36
00:02:26,085 --> 00:02:31,854
Think of availability threats as existing on a scale, from "annoying" to "catastrophic." At the

37
00:02:31,854 --> 00:02:34,931
bottom of that scale: a single pod crashes.

38
00:02:34,931 --> 00:02:40,700
That's routine, and Kubernetes already handles it by restarting the container — barely worth calling

39
00:02:40,700 --> 00:02:42,623
an availability incident at all.

40
00:02:42,623 --> 00:02:48,392
One level up: an entire node dies — the physical or virtual machine underneath several

41
00:02:48,392 --> 00:02:49,931
pods disappears at once.

42
00:02:49,931 --> 00:02:55,700
Above that: an entire availability zone, essentially a whole data center, goes offline — a

43
00:02:55,700 --> 00:03:01,469
genuinely rare event, but one that has happened to every major cloud provider, including AWS,

44
00:03:01,469 --> 00:03:02,623
more than once.

45
00:03:02,623 --> 00:03:08,392
And at the very top of that scale: sustained, unpredictable traffic that has nothing to

46
00:03:08,392 --> 00:03:14,162
do with hardware failing at all, but simply exceeds what your current capacity can absorb,

47
00:03:14,162 --> 00:03:19,931
which — if you're not prepared for it — is functionally indistinguishable from an outage

48
00:03:19,931 --> 00:03:24,546
to the person trying to use your product at that exact moment.

49
00:03:24,546 --> 00:03:29,546
Every technique in this phase defends against one specific rung of that ladder.

50
00:03:29,546 --> 00:03:33,777
Multi-AZ RDS and topology spread constraints defend against the AZ-failure rung.

51
00:03:33,777 --> 00:03:39,546
Redis persistence defends against a narrower, sneakier threat: not availability exactly, but data durability when

52
00:03:39,546 --> 00:03:42,238
a pod restarts for entirely mundane reasons.

53
00:03:42,238 --> 00:03:45,315
HPA and Karpenter defend against the traffic-spike rung.

54
00:03:45,315 --> 00:03:51,085
PodDisruptionBudgets defend against a threat that's easy to forget entirely: your own maintenance work accidentally

55
00:03:51,085 --> 00:03:52,238
becoming the outage.

56
00:03:52,238 --> 00:03:58,008
Read replicas defend against a slow, creeping form of unavailability — a database quietly buckling

57
00:03:58,008 --> 00:04:01,469
under read load until every query becomes agonizingly slow.

58
00:04:01,469 --> 00:04:07,238
And chaos engineering, backup restore testing, and SLO monitoring aren't really separate defenses at all

59
00:04:07,238 --> 00:04:13,008
— they're how you verify, honestly and repeatedly, that everything above them actually works the

60
00:04:13,008 --> 00:04:14,931
way you're claiming it does.

61
00:04:14,931 --> 00:04:20,700
One quick, important detour before we start building, because it changes how you should read

62
00:04:20,700 --> 00:04:22,623
every claim in this phase.

63
00:04:22,623 --> 00:04:28,392
In SOC 2 language, availability doesn't mean "always perfectly up." It means you've defined a

64
00:04:28,392 --> 00:04:34,162
specific, measurable service level objective — say, 99.9 percent uptime over a rolling month —

65
00:04:34,162 --> 00:04:39,931
and you can demonstrate, with real evidence, that you're meeting it, or that you know

66
00:04:39,931 --> 00:04:42,238
exactly when and why you didn't.

67
00:04:42,238 --> 00:04:48,008
Ninety-nine point nine percent, done honestly, still leaves you about forty-three minutes of allowed downtime

68
00:04:48,008 --> 00:04:49,162
every single month.

69
00:04:49,162 --> 00:04:52,623
That's not a loophole — it's the entire point.

70
00:04:52,623 --> 00:04:58,008
Nobody, including AWS itself, promises zero downtime, because that promise would be a lie.

71
00:04:58,008 --> 00:05:03,777
What SOC 2 actually wants from you is intellectual honesty: a stated target, real measurement

72
00:05:03,777 --> 00:05:09,546
against that target, and a demonstrated ability to recover quickly when something breaks — not

73
00:05:09,546 --> 00:05:11,085
a fantasy of perfection.

74
00:05:11,085 --> 00:05:16,854
One more piece of vocabulary worth nailing down before we start building, because you'll hear

75
00:05:16,854 --> 00:05:22,623
both terms constantly for the rest of this phase, and auditors use them with real

76
00:05:22,623 --> 00:05:23,777
precision, not loosely.

77
00:05:23,777 --> 00:05:29,546
RTO — Recovery Time Objective — is how long you're allowed to be down before

78
00:05:29,546 --> 00:05:31,854
you've failed your own stated commitment.

79
00:05:31,854 --> 00:05:37,623
RPO — Recovery Point Objective — is how much data you're allowed to lose, measured

80
00:05:37,623 --> 00:05:42,238
in time, if a failure happens right in the middle of things.

81
00:05:42,238 --> 00:05:48,008
A four-hour RTO means "we commit to being fully back up within four hours of

82
00:05:48,008 --> 00:05:53,777
a serious incident." A five-minute RPO means "in the worst case, we might lose up

83
00:05:53,777 --> 00:05:59,546
to five minutes of the very latest data, but never more than that." These two

84
00:05:59,546 --> 00:06:05,315
numbers aren't abstract compliance jargon — they're the actual yardsticks every single technique in this

85
00:06:05,315 --> 00:06:06,854
phase gets measured against.

86
00:06:06,854 --> 00:06:12,623
Multi-AZ failover, done right, gives you an RTO measured in minutes and an RPO close

87
00:06:12,623 --> 00:06:16,085
to zero, because the standby was never meaningfully behind.

88
00:06:16,085 --> 00:06:21,854
Our quarterly restore test, later in this phase, exists specifically to prove your stated RTO

89
00:06:21,854 --> 00:06:27,623
for full disaster recovery is actually true, rather than a comforting number someone wrote in

90
00:06:27,623 --> 00:06:30,315
a document once and never actually tested.

91
00:06:30,315 --> 00:06:36,085
Let's start at the foundation, the same place the hospital's electricians would start: the single

92
00:06:36,085 --> 00:06:41,854
most important piece of equipment in the whole building, and what happens the instant its

93
00:06:41,854 --> 00:06:43,008
room goes dark.

94
00:06:43,008 --> 00:06:46,469
Here's a genuinely uncomfortable thought experiment to open with.

95
00:06:46,469 --> 00:06:52,238
Suppose your entire application — beautifully engineered, perfectly logged, immaculately secured — runs happily for

96
00:06:52,238 --> 00:06:55,315
a year in a single AWS Availability Zone.

97
00:06:55,315 --> 00:06:57,623
One day, that AZ goes down.

98
00:06:57,623 --> 00:06:58,777
Not your fault.

99
00:06:58,777 --> 00:07:00,700
Not a bug you wrote.

100
00:07:00,700 --> 00:07:06,469
Just a data center, hundreds of miles away from anything you control, losing power or

101
00:07:06,469 --> 00:07:08,008
cooling or network connectivity.

102
00:07:08,008 --> 00:07:13,777
Your system is now completely down, for as long as that AZ stays down, and

103
00:07:13,777 --> 00:07:19,546
there was nothing in your architecture that could have prevented it — because your architecture

104
00:07:19,546 --> 00:07:24,162
never gave itself the option of surviving without that one specific building.

105
00:07:24,162 --> 00:07:29,931
This is the single point of failure problem, and it's worth understanding why it's so

106
00:07:29,931 --> 00:07:31,469
seductive to accidentally build.

107
00:07:31,469 --> 00:07:33,392
A single-AZ system is simpler.

108
00:07:33,392 --> 00:07:34,162
It's cheaper.

109
00:07:34,162 --> 00:07:39,931
It works perfectly in every test you run, right up until the one day it

110
00:07:39,931 --> 00:07:45,700
doesn't — and that one day is precisely the day your availability SLO, and your

111
00:07:45,700 --> 00:07:48,777
credibility with your customers, gets tested for real.

112
00:07:48,777 --> 00:07:54,546
Let's fix this at the database layer first, because if your database is down, nothing

113
00:07:54,546 --> 00:08:00,315
else in your architecture matters at all — every other service in the stack is

114
00:08:00,315 --> 00:08:01,854
just waiting on it.

115
00:08:01,854 --> 00:08:07,623
RDS Multi-AZ solves this with a technique that's elegant once you understand it: AWS maintains

116
00:08:07,623 --> 00:08:13,392
a synchronous standby replica of your primary database, physically located in a different Availability Zone.

117
00:08:13,392 --> 00:08:19,162
"Synchronous" is the key word — every single write to your primary database is also

118
00:08:19,162 --> 00:08:24,931
confirmed on the standby before your application even receives an acknowledgment that the write succeeded.

119
00:08:24,931 --> 00:08:30,700
This matters enormously, and it's worth contrasting with the read replicas we'll build later in

120
00:08:30,700 --> 00:08:34,162
this phase, which replicate asynchronously and can lag behind.

121
00:08:34,162 --> 00:08:36,469
The Multi-AZ standby is never behind.

122
00:08:36,469 --> 00:08:42,238
It is, at every moment, a mirror-perfect copy of your primary, just sitting in a

123
00:08:42,238 --> 00:08:46,085
different building, ready to take over the instant it's needed.

124
00:08:46,085 --> 00:08:51,854
When the primary fails — hardware issue, an AZ-wide outage, whatever the cause — AWS

125
00:08:51,854 --> 00:08:57,623
detects it and automatically promotes the standby to become the new primary, typically completing that

126
00:08:57,623 --> 00:08:59,546
failover in under two minutes.

127
00:08:59,546 --> 00:09:02,238
Your application doesn't need custom failover logic.

128
00:09:02,238 --> 00:09:08,008
It reconnects to the same database endpoint — AWS handles the DNS-level redirection underneath —

129
00:09:08,008 --> 00:09:13,777
and after a brief connection interruption, it's talking to what is now the primary database,

130
00:09:13,777 --> 00:09:19,546
seamlessly, with zero data loss, because the standby was never out of sync in the

131
00:09:19,546 --> 00:09:20,315
first place.

132
00:09:20,315 --> 00:09:21,469
On screen: terraform/modules/rds/main.tf.

133
00:09:21,469 --> 00:09:27,238
The setting that does all this work is a single boolean, multi_az, set to true

134
00:09:27,238 --> 00:09:28,008
for production.

135
00:09:28,008 --> 00:09:33,392
Notice we also explicitly set availability_zone to null — a small but meaningful detail.

136
00:09:33,392 --> 00:09:39,162
If you pin the primary to a specific AZ, you're quietly reintroducing bias into a

137
00:09:39,162 --> 00:09:44,931
system that's supposed to be AZ-agnostic; letting AWS choose keeps your infrastructure honest about not

138
00:09:44,931 --> 00:09:47,238
depending on any one particular zone.

139
00:09:47,238 --> 00:09:53,008
We add deletion_protection, because an accidentally deleted production database is a self-inflicted availability incident that

140
00:09:53,008 --> 00:09:58,777
Multi-AZ cannot save you from — no standby in the world helps if a human

141
00:09:58,777 --> 00:10:00,700
being deletes the whole instance.

142
00:10:00,700 --> 00:10:06,469
And we set a thirty-day backup retention window with automated snapshots, which becomes relevant again

143
00:10:06,469 --> 00:10:10,315
later in this phase, when we get to restore testing.

144
00:10:10,315 --> 00:10:14,546
We also attach a CloudWatch alarm specifically watching for failover events.

145
00:10:14,546 --> 00:10:20,315
Here's a subtlety worth sitting with: Multi-AZ failover being fast and automatic is genuinely good

146
00:10:20,315 --> 00:10:25,315
news for your uptime, but it should never be invisible to your team.

147
00:10:25,315 --> 00:10:31,085
A failover happening means something actually failed — maybe transiently, maybe not — and if

148
00:10:31,085 --> 00:10:36,854
failovers start happening every week instead of once a year, that's a serious underlying infrastructure

149
00:10:36,854 --> 00:10:38,392
problem quietly announcing itself.

150
00:10:38,392 --> 00:10:44,162
The alarm ensures a human finds out, even though the system recovered on its own

151
00:10:44,162 --> 00:10:45,315
without needing one.

152
00:10:45,315 --> 00:10:49,931
With the database covered, let's move up a layer, to Kubernetes itself.

153
00:10:49,931 --> 00:10:55,700
Here's something that surprises a lot of engineers the first time they hear it: the

154
00:10:55,700 --> 00:11:01,469
EKS control plane — the brain coordinating your entire cluster — is already Multi-AZ by

155
00:11:01,469 --> 00:11:02,169
default.

156
00:11:02,169 --> 00:11:07,554
AWS manages that redundancy for you, and there's no configuration required on your part.

157
00:11:07,554 --> 00:11:13,323
What is entirely your responsibility, and what most teams get wrong by accident rather than

158
00:11:13,323 --> 00:11:19,092
on purpose, is where your worker nodes and your actual application pods end up running.

159
00:11:19,092 --> 00:11:20,246
On screen: terraform/modules/eks/main.tf.

160
00:11:20,246 --> 00:11:26,015
The node group configuration specifies subnet_ids drawn from all three private subnets — one per

161
00:11:26,015 --> 00:11:29,862
Availability Zone — rather than pointing at a single subnet.

162
00:11:29,862 --> 00:11:34,477
This alone doesn't guarantee even distribution; it just makes even distribution possible.

163
00:11:34,477 --> 00:11:40,246
We set a desired size of six nodes specifically because six divides evenly by three

164
00:11:40,246 --> 00:11:46,015
AZs, giving Kubernetes a clean, balanced number to distribute — an oddly satisfying but genuinely

165
00:11:46,015 --> 00:11:50,246
practical bit of arithmetic that saves you from lopsided placement later.

166
00:11:50,246 --> 00:11:56,015
But here's the trap, and it's worth naming explicitly, because it's the most common Multi-AZ

167
00:11:56,015 --> 00:11:59,477
mistake in the entire industry, not just this course.

168
00:11:59,477 --> 00:12:05,246
Having nodes spread across three AZs does absolutely nothing, by itself, to guarantee your pods

169
00:12:05,246 --> 00:12:07,938
are spread across those same three AZs.

170
00:12:07,938 --> 00:12:13,708
Kubernetes' default scheduler is optimizing for resource efficiency — bin-packing pods onto nodes efficiently —

171
00:12:13,708 --> 00:12:15,246
not for AZ diversity.

172
00:12:15,246 --> 00:12:21,015
Left to its own devices, it will quite happily place all six replicas of your

173
00:12:21,015 --> 00:12:26,785
API deployment onto nodes that all happen to sit in the same Availability Zone, if

174
00:12:26,785 --> 00:12:29,862
that's what looks most resource-efficient at scheduling time.

175
00:12:29,862 --> 00:12:35,631
You'd have three healthy AZs of infrastructure and one giant, invisible single point of failure

176
00:12:35,631 --> 00:12:40,631
sitting right in the middle of it, disguised as a "highly available" deployment.

177
00:12:40,631 --> 00:12:43,708
This is exactly what topologySpreadConstraints exist to prevent.

178
00:12:43,708 --> 00:12:44,862
On screen: helm/templates/api-deployment.yaml.

179
00:12:44,862 --> 00:12:50,631
The first constraint uses topology.kubernetes.io/zone as its key, with maxSkew set to 1 — meaning

180
00:12:50,631 --> 00:12:56,400
the difference between the AZ with the most replicas and the AZ with the fewest

181
00:12:56,400 --> 00:13:02,169
can never exceed one pod — and whenUnsatisfiable set to DoNotSchedule, which is the strict,

182
00:13:02,169 --> 00:13:07,938
uncompromising setting: if honoring this constraint isn't possible right now, the pod simply stays pending

183
00:13:07,938 --> 00:13:11,400
rather than landing somewhere that violates our spread requirement.

184
00:13:11,400 --> 00:13:17,169
A second, softer constraint spreads pods across individual nodes within each zone too, using ScheduleAnyway

185
00:13:17,169 --> 00:13:22,938
instead, because a slightly uneven distribution within a single zone is a minor inefficiency, not

186
00:13:22,938 --> 00:13:26,015
a genuine availability risk worth blocking scheduling over.

187
00:13:26,015 --> 00:13:31,785
We add one more layer on top: pod anti-affinity, specifically discouraging — though not strictly

188
00:13:31,785 --> 00:13:36,785
forbidding — two API replicas from landing on the exact same physical node.

189
00:13:36,785 --> 00:13:39,862
Why does this matter separately from AZ spread?

190
00:13:39,862 --> 00:13:45,631
Because a single node dying is a far more common event than an entire AZ

191
00:13:45,631 --> 00:13:46,331
dying.

192
00:13:46,331 --> 00:13:52,100
If four of your six API replicas happen to be sharing one large node, that

193
00:13:52,100 --> 00:13:57,869
one node's failure takes out two-thirds of your API capacity in one shot, even though

194
00:13:57,869 --> 00:14:01,331
your AZ-level spread constraint was satisfied the whole time.

195
00:14:01,331 --> 00:14:04,792
AZ spread protects you from the rare, catastrophic event.

196
00:14:04,792 --> 00:14:09,792
Node-level anti-affinity protects you from the far more mundane, far more frequent one.

197
00:14:09,792 --> 00:14:15,562
Let's verify all of this actually worked, because — and this is a lesson worth

198
00:14:15,562 --> 00:14:21,331
internalizing early in this entire phase — a Terraform apply completing successfully and a kubectl

199
00:14:21,331 --> 00:14:25,177
apply returning "deployment configured" tell you your configuration was accepted.

200
00:14:25,177 --> 00:14:29,023
Neither one tells you your system is actually distributed correctly.

201
00:14:29,023 --> 00:14:32,485
Verification is its own separate, mandatory step, every time.

202
00:14:32,485 --> 00:14:38,254
On screen: aws rds describe-db-instances, querying specifically for the MultiAZ field, which should return true.

203
00:14:38,254 --> 00:14:44,023
Then a query for which specific AZ currently holds the primary — useful context, not

204
00:14:44,023 --> 00:14:47,100
a pass or fail signal on its own.

205
00:14:47,100 --> 00:14:52,869
Then, on the Kubernetes side: kubectl get nodes, piped through jq to extract each node's

206
00:14:52,869 --> 00:14:55,177
zone label, then sorted and counted.

207
00:14:55,177 --> 00:15:00,946
You're looking for something close to an even three-way split — two nodes per zone,

208
00:15:00,946 --> 00:15:06,331
roughly, not five nodes crammed into one zone and one node lonely in another.

209
00:15:06,331 --> 00:15:12,100
Finally, the check that actually matters most for real-world resilience: querying pod placement specifically for

210
00:15:12,100 --> 00:15:17,869
your API deployment, confirming that your topology spread constraint didn't just get accepted by the

211
00:15:17,869 --> 00:15:23,638
API server, but is genuinely holding in practice, right now, on your actual running cluster.

212
00:15:23,638 --> 00:15:29,408
Here's your first checkpoint for this phase, and it's the most important one, because everything

213
00:15:29,408 --> 00:15:31,715
after it builds on this foundation.

214
00:15:31,715 --> 00:15:34,792
Confirm your RDS instance reports Multi-AZ as true.

215
00:15:34,792 --> 00:15:38,254
Confirm your nodes are evenly spread across three zones.

216
00:15:38,254 --> 00:15:43,254
Confirm your API pods are evenly spread too — not just your nodes.

217
00:15:43,254 --> 00:15:49,023
And then do the genuinely useful part: pick one Availability Zone, and cordon every node

218
00:15:49,023 --> 00:15:52,100
in it, simulating that entire zone going dark.

219
00:15:52,100 --> 00:15:55,177
Watch what actually happens to your running pods.

220
00:15:55,177 --> 00:15:58,638
Do they reschedule cleanly onto the remaining two zones?

221
00:15:58,638 --> 00:16:00,562
How long does that take?

222
00:16:00,562 --> 00:16:06,331
Document it, because "I configured Multi-AZ" and "I watched Multi-AZ actually save my system during

223
00:16:06,331 --> 00:16:12,100
a simulated failure" are two very different sentences to be able to say to an

224
00:16:12,100 --> 00:16:15,946
auditor — and only one of them is genuinely convincing.

225
00:16:15,946 --> 00:16:21,715
Let's make this concrete against our own two systems rather than talking about it only

226
00:16:21,715 --> 00:16:22,869
in the abstract.

227
00:16:22,869 --> 00:16:28,638
Financial RAG's production database, financial-rag-prod, is exactly the instance we just configured — Multi-AZ, deletion

228
00:16:28,638 --> 00:16:32,485
protection, thirty-day backups, the CloudWatch failover alarm, all of it.

229
00:16:32,485 --> 00:16:38,254
RiskOracle runs its own separate RDS instance, holding portfolio risk calculations rather than SEC filing

230
00:16:38,254 --> 00:16:44,023
embeddings, and it gets the identical treatment, not a lighter version because it feels like

231
00:16:44,023 --> 00:16:45,562
the less customer-facing system.

232
00:16:45,562 --> 00:16:51,331
Cordon a zone against both instances, not just one, and watch each one fail over

233
00:16:51,331 --> 00:16:52,031
independently.

234
00:16:52,031 --> 00:16:57,800
If you've only ever tested this against financial-rag, you don't actually know RiskOracle survives an

235
00:16:57,800 --> 00:17:02,415
AZ failure — you're assuming it does because the Terraform looks similar.

236
00:17:02,415 --> 00:17:08,185
Before we move to the next lecture, a quick, honest aside about the two most

237
00:17:08,185 --> 00:17:13,954
common ways teams mess up exactly what we just built, because I'd rather you hear

238
00:17:13,954 --> 00:17:18,185
about these now than discover them during an actual AZ outage.

239
00:17:18,185 --> 00:17:23,954
The first mistake: nailing the AZ spread for your main API deployment, and then quietly

240
00:17:23,954 --> 00:17:28,954
forgetting to apply the same discipline to every other component in the system.

241
00:17:28,954 --> 00:17:30,108
Your agent workers.

242
00:17:30,108 --> 00:17:31,646
Your background job processors.

243
00:17:31,646 --> 00:17:33,569
Any sidecar or supporting service.

244
00:17:33,569 --> 00:17:39,338
If your API is beautifully spread across three zones but your job queue processor is

245
00:17:39,338 --> 00:17:45,108
a single replica sitting in one zone with no spread constraint at all, you haven't

246
00:17:45,108 --> 00:17:50,877
actually eliminated your single point of failure — you've just moved it to a less

247
00:17:50,877 --> 00:17:56,646
obvious place, one that's easy to forget about entirely until the exact AZ holding it

248
00:17:56,646 --> 00:17:59,723
happens to be the one that goes down.

249
00:17:59,723 --> 00:18:05,492
The second mistake is subtler, and it's about StatefulSets specifically — which matters because we're

250
00:18:05,492 --> 00:18:08,954
about to build one, in the very next lecture.

251
00:18:08,954 --> 00:18:14,723
Deployments reschedule freely; if a pod dies, Kubernetes creates a fresh replacement wherever makes sense.

252
00:18:14,723 --> 00:18:20,492
StatefulSets, by design, are far more attached to their identity and their storage volume, which

253
00:18:20,492 --> 00:18:26,262
is exactly why they're used for stateful things in the first place — but it

254
00:18:26,262 --> 00:18:32,031
also means AZ spread constraints on a StatefulSet interact with storage in ways that trip

255
00:18:32,031 --> 00:18:32,800
people up.

256
00:18:32,800 --> 00:18:38,569
An Amazon EBS volume lives in one specific Availability Zone, permanently, for its entire life.

257
00:18:38,569 --> 00:18:44,338
If your StatefulSet pod gets rescheduled onto a node in a different zone than the

258
00:18:44,338 --> 00:18:50,108
one holding its volume, it simply cannot reattach to its own data — it will

259
00:18:50,108 --> 00:18:55,877
sit there, pending, forever, looking every bit like your system successfully "recovered" from a failure

260
00:18:55,877 --> 00:18:58,954
while actually silently failing to start at all.

261
00:18:58,954 --> 00:19:03,954
Keep that specific trap in mind, because it's about to become directly relevant.

262
00:19:03,954 --> 00:19:09,723
Let's talk about a kind of failure that's easy to underestimate, precisely because it happens

263
00:19:09,723 --> 00:19:13,569
constantly and is almost always harmless: a pod simply restarts.

264
00:19:13,569 --> 00:19:14,723
Maybe it crashed.

265
00:19:14,723 --> 00:19:18,569
Maybe Kubernetes evicted it to make room for something else.

266
00:19:18,569 --> 00:19:20,877
Maybe you deployed a new version.

267
00:19:20,877 --> 00:19:26,646
For a stateless API pod, a restart is a non-event — a fresh copy starts

268
00:19:26,646 --> 00:19:32,415
up, reconnects to the database, and nothing was ever lost, because nothing important was ever

269
00:19:32,415 --> 00:19:35,108
stored inside that pod to begin with.

270
00:19:35,108 --> 00:19:40,877
Redis is a different animal entirely, and here's the analogy that makes the danger click

271
00:19:40,877 --> 00:19:46,646
immediately: think of default, unconfigured Redis as a brilliant colleague with absolutely no long-term memory.

272
00:19:46,646 --> 00:19:52,415
Ask them anything during the workday, and they'll answer instantly, from memory, faster than anyone

273
00:19:52,415 --> 00:19:53,954
else in the building.

274
00:19:53,954 --> 00:19:59,338
But the moment they step out for lunch and come back, they remember nothing.

275
00:19:59,338 --> 00:20:00,877
Not who you are.

276
00:20:00,877 --> 00:20:03,569
Not what you discussed an hour ago.

277
00:20:03,569 --> 00:20:05,877
Every conversation starts completely from scratch.

278
00:20:05,877 --> 00:20:11,646
For some workloads, that's genuinely fine — a rate-limiting counter that resets itself is barely

279
00:20:11,646 --> 00:20:12,415
worth mourning.

280
00:20:12,415 --> 00:20:18,185
But for agent state, session continuity, and query result caching, quietly losing everything on every

281
00:20:18,185 --> 00:20:20,877
routine pod restart isn't a minor inconvenience.

282
00:20:20,877 --> 00:20:26,646
It's a real, repeated, entirely avoidable degradation of your product, happening every single time Kubernetes

283
00:20:26,646 --> 00:20:31,262
does something completely ordinary and unremarkable, like rolling out a routine update.

284
00:20:31,262 --> 00:20:37,031
Redis solves its own amnesia with something called AOF — Append Only File — persistence,

285
00:20:37,031 --> 00:20:42,800
and the underlying idea is refreshingly close to what we built for audit logging back

286
00:20:42,800 --> 00:20:44,338
in an earlier phase.

287
00:20:44,338 --> 00:20:50,108
Every single write operation gets appended to a log file on disk, in the exact

288
00:20:50,108 --> 00:20:51,262
order it happened.

289
00:20:51,262 --> 00:20:57,031
When Redis restarts, rather than starting with a blank memory, it reads through that entire

290
00:20:57,031 --> 00:21:02,800
log from the beginning and replays every operation, exactly as it happened, reconstructing its complete

291
00:21:02,800 --> 00:21:06,262
in-memory state before it starts serving any new requests.

292
00:21:06,262 --> 00:21:12,031
It's less like a colleague getting their memory wiped, and more like a colleague who,

293
00:21:12,031 --> 00:21:17,800
every single time they step away, immediately and instinctively re-reads their own meticulous notebook the

294
00:21:17,800 --> 00:21:23,569
moment they return — and instead of "what did we talk about," they're right back

295
00:21:23,569 --> 00:21:25,492
up to speed in seconds.

296
00:21:25,492 --> 00:21:26,646
Let's configure it.

297
00:21:26,646 --> 00:21:27,800
On screen: helm/templates/redis-statefulset.yaml.

298
00:21:27,800 --> 00:21:33,569
First, notice this is a StatefulSet, not a Deployment — directly connecting to the caution

299
00:21:33,569 --> 00:21:39,338
from a moment ago, because Redis, once we give it persistent data on disk, needs

300
00:21:39,338 --> 00:21:45,108
a stable identity and a stable volume, exactly the kind of stateful component that AZ-and-storage

301
00:21:45,108 --> 00:21:48,954
mismatch trap can quietly break if you're not paying attention.

302
00:21:48,954 --> 00:21:52,800
The persistence configuration itself lives in the container's startup arguments.

303
00:21:52,800 --> 00:21:58,569
appendonly yes turns AOF on at all — without this single flag, everything else in

304
00:21:58,569 --> 00:22:02,800
this section is meaningless, because Redis defaults to pure in-memory operation.

305
00:22:02,800 --> 00:22:08,569
appendfsync everysec is the setting that actually determines your real durability guarantee, and it's worth

306
00:22:08,569 --> 00:22:14,338
understanding the trade-off explicitly rather than trusting the default blindly: Redis batches writes to disk

307
00:22:14,338 --> 00:22:17,800
once per second, rather than after every single command.

308
00:22:17,800 --> 00:22:23,569
This is a deliberate, reasonable compromise — syncing after literally every write would be dramatically

309
00:22:23,569 --> 00:22:29,338
safer but would also devastate performance, turning Redis from "blazing fast in-memory store" into "a

310
00:22:29,338 --> 00:22:35,108
fairly ordinary disk-bound database wearing Redis's name." Syncing once a second means that, in the

311
00:22:35,108 --> 00:22:40,877
absolute worst case — the exact instant of a hard crash, mid-second — you could

312
00:22:40,877 --> 00:22:45,492
lose up to roughly one second's worth of the most recent writes.

313
00:22:45,492 --> 00:22:50,492
For a caching and agent-state layer, that's a genuinely sensible trade to make.

314
00:22:50,492 --> 00:22:56,262
For something like financial transaction data, that same trade-off would likely be unacceptable, and you'd

315
00:22:56,262 --> 00:23:02,031
want a different tool entirely, or a stricter, always-sync setting despite the performance cost —

316
00:23:02,031 --> 00:23:07,800
which is exactly the kind of judgment call worth making deliberately, not by leaving whatever

317
00:23:07,800 --> 00:23:09,723
the default happens to be.

318
00:23:09,723 --> 00:23:15,492
We also configure automatic AOF rewriting, triggered once the file has grown by 100 percent

319
00:23:15,492 --> 00:23:21,262
since the last rewrite, with a 64-megabyte minimum size before that trigger even becomes relevant.

320
00:23:21,262 --> 00:23:27,031
Without this, the append-only log would grow forever, unboundedly, because it never removes old, superseded

321
00:23:27,031 --> 00:23:32,800
entries on its own — rewriting periodically compacts it down to just the operations needed

322
00:23:32,800 --> 00:23:38,569
to reconstruct current state, similar in spirit to how we handled data retention with table

323
00:23:38,569 --> 00:23:41,262
partitioning back in the audit logging phase.

324
00:23:41,262 --> 00:23:47,031
And as a genuine belt-and-suspenders backup, we layer in periodic RDB snapshots too — full

325
00:23:47,031 --> 00:23:52,800
point-in-time memory dumps, taken on three different schedules depending on how much write activity has

326
00:23:52,800 --> 00:23:58,569
occurred, giving you a second, independent recovery path if the AOF log itself were ever

327
00:23:58,569 --> 00:23:59,338
somehow corrupted.

328
00:23:59,338 --> 00:24:05,108
Notice the storage class specifically: gp3-encrypted, backed by a persistent volume claim requesting fifty gigabytes.

329
00:24:05,108 --> 00:24:10,877
This is the actual physical disk AOF writes are landing on, and it's what makes

330
00:24:10,877 --> 00:24:16,646
the whole scheme work at all — without a persistent volume surviving the pod's own

331
00:24:16,646 --> 00:24:22,415
lifecycle, "append to disk" would just mean appending to storage that vanishes right alongside the

332
00:24:22,415 --> 00:24:25,492
pod, which would make the entire exercise pointless.

333
00:24:25,492 --> 00:24:28,954
Losing data on a crash is one failure mode.

334
00:24:28,954 --> 00:24:34,723
Losing the entire underlying volume — a corrupted disk, an accidental deletion, a far worse

335
00:24:34,723 --> 00:24:40,492
day than a routine restart — is a different, more serious one, and AOF alone

336
00:24:40,492 --> 00:24:42,031
doesn't protect against it.

337
00:24:42,031 --> 00:24:44,338
That's what the backup CronJob handles.

338
00:24:44,338 --> 00:24:47,031
On screen: k8s/redis-backup.yaml, running every six hours.

339
00:24:47,031 --> 00:24:52,800
It triggers a BGSAVE — a background snapshot that doesn't block Redis from continuing to

340
00:24:52,800 --> 00:24:58,569
serve live traffic while it's being taken — waits for it to complete, then copies

341
00:24:58,569 --> 00:25:04,338
the resulting dump file to S3, directly into Glacier storage class, since these are backups

342
00:25:04,338 --> 00:25:09,723
you hope to never actually need, making cold, cheap storage the obviously correct choice.

343
00:25:09,723 --> 00:25:15,492
It also prunes anything older than thirty days, keeping cost under control rather than accumulating

344
00:25:15,492 --> 00:25:16,262
backups indefinitely.

345
00:25:16,262 --> 00:25:18,569
Configuration and good intentions aren't evidence.

346
00:25:18,569 --> 00:25:21,262
Let's actually watch data survive a restart.

347
00:25:21,262 --> 00:25:26,646
On screen: we write a test key directly with redis-cli — SET test-key "important-value".

348
00:25:26,646 --> 00:25:32,415
Then we do something that would have been genuinely catastrophic against a default, non-persistent Redis:

349
00:25:32,415 --> 00:25:37,415
we forcibly delete the pod outright, not a graceful restart, a hard delete.

350
00:25:37,415 --> 00:25:43,185
Kubernetes, because this is a StatefulSet, creates a fresh replacement pod with the same identity

351
00:25:43,185 --> 00:25:45,492
and reattaches the same persistent volume.

352
00:25:45,492 --> 00:25:49,723
We wait for it to report ready, then run GET test-key.

353
00:25:49,723 --> 00:25:55,492
If AOF is genuinely configured and working, that value comes back exactly as we set

354
00:25:55,492 --> 00:26:01,262
it — "important-value" — recovered entirely from the replayed log on disk, not from some

355
00:26:01,262 --> 00:26:05,877
memory that magically survived, because nothing about a pod deletion preserves memory.

356
00:26:05,877 --> 00:26:11,646
If it comes back empty, something in this configuration is wrong, and you've found that

357
00:26:11,646 --> 00:26:17,415
out now, in a deliberate test, rather than during a real incident when it actually

358
00:26:17,415 --> 00:26:18,115
mattered.

359
00:26:18,115 --> 00:26:20,423
Here's your checkpoint for this lecture.

360
00:26:20,423 --> 00:26:23,500
Deploy Redis with AOF persistence exactly as configured.

361
00:26:23,500 --> 00:26:29,269
Write test data, then forcibly delete the pod — not a graceful kubectl rollout restart,

362
00:26:29,269 --> 00:26:33,115
an actual hard delete — and confirm your data survives.

363
00:26:33,115 --> 00:26:38,885
Then separately verify the backup CronJob is actually running on schedule and producing real, retrievable

364
00:26:38,885 --> 00:26:44,654
objects in S3, because a backup job that silently fails every six hours, quietly, for

365
00:26:44,654 --> 00:26:50,423
months, is arguably worse than having no backup job at all — it gives you

366
00:26:50,423 --> 00:26:56,192
false confidence right up until the exact moment you desperately need it to have actually

367
00:26:56,192 --> 00:26:56,962
been working.

368
00:26:56,962 --> 00:27:02,731
Financial RAG's Redis instance caches embedding lookups and recent query results — lose it, and

369
00:27:02,731 --> 00:27:08,500
the system gets slower for a few minutes while the cache rebuilds, genuinely annoying but

370
00:27:08,500 --> 00:27:09,269
not catastrophic.

371
00:27:09,269 --> 00:27:15,038
RiskOracle's Redis instance is a different animal: it caches intermediate risk calculations mid-pipeline, and losing

372
00:27:15,038 --> 00:27:20,808
an in-flight calculation without AOF persistence means silently re-running a computation that may have already

373
00:27:20,808 --> 00:27:24,269
fed into a report someone downstream is relying on.

374
00:27:24,269 --> 00:27:30,038
Run the exact same hard-delete test against RiskOracle's Redis pod that you just ran against

375
00:27:30,038 --> 00:27:30,808
Financial RAG's.

376
00:27:30,808 --> 00:27:36,577
If RiskOracle's Redis was ever deployed from an older Helm values file without the AOF

377
00:27:36,577 --> 00:27:42,346
settings we just walked through, this is precisely the moment you'll find out, before an

378
00:27:42,346 --> 00:27:43,115
auditor does.

379
00:27:43,115 --> 00:27:48,885
Let's shift from "surviving failure" to a genuinely different kind of threat, one that has

380
00:27:48,885 --> 00:27:51,962
nothing to do with anything breaking at all.

381
00:27:51,962 --> 00:27:57,731
Imagine your hospital, generators humming perfectly, both buildings fully powered — and then a bus

382
00:27:57,731 --> 00:28:03,500
crash happens three blocks away, and forty patients arrive in the emergency room within twenty

383
00:28:03,500 --> 00:28:06,192
minutes, ten times the normal Tuesday-afternoon volume.

384
00:28:06,192 --> 00:28:06,962
Nothing failed.

385
00:28:06,962 --> 00:28:08,885
The building is completely intact.

386
00:28:08,885 --> 00:28:14,654
But if staffing doesn't scale to meet that sudden, legitimate demand, patients wait, care suffers,

387
00:28:14,654 --> 00:28:20,423
and from the outside, it looks exactly like an outage, even though every generator is

388
00:28:20,423 --> 00:28:21,192
running flawlessly.

389
00:28:21,192 --> 00:28:22,731
That's a traffic spike.

390
00:28:22,731 --> 00:28:28,500
Not a hardware failure, not an AZ going dark — just far more legitimate demand

391
00:28:28,500 --> 00:28:31,577
than your current capacity was provisioned to handle.

392
00:28:31,577 --> 00:28:37,346
And the standard, blunt-instrument way to defend against it — simply provisioning for worst-case load,

393
00:28:37,346 --> 00:28:43,115
permanently, all the time — is staggeringly wasteful, paying peak-capacity prices twenty-four hours a day

394
00:28:43,115 --> 00:28:48,115
for a spike that might only materialize a handful of afternoons a year.

395
00:28:48,115 --> 00:28:53,885
The right answer is elasticity: capacity that grows automatically when demand actually shows up, and

396
00:28:53,885 --> 00:28:59,654
shrinks back down once it passes, without needing a human being to notice and manually

397
00:28:59,654 --> 00:29:01,962
intervene at two in the morning.

398
00:29:01,962 --> 00:29:07,731
In Kubernetes, that elasticity operates on two genuinely distinct layers, and it's worth being precise

399
00:29:07,731 --> 00:29:12,346
about the difference, because conflating them is a common source of confusion.

400
00:29:12,346 --> 00:29:17,731
The Horizontal Pod Autoscaler, HPA, controls how many copies of your application are running.

401
00:29:17,731 --> 00:29:23,115
Karpenter controls how many physical nodes exist underneath those copies in the first place.

402
00:29:23,115 --> 00:29:28,885
HPA can decide it wants twenty API replicas running, but if your existing nodes don't

403
00:29:28,885 --> 00:29:34,654
have room for that many pods, those extra replicas just sit pending, uselessly, until something

404
00:29:34,654 --> 00:29:36,577
provisions more room for them.

405
00:29:36,577 --> 00:29:42,346
You need both layers working in concert — more staff, and more space for that

406
00:29:42,346 --> 00:29:48,115
staff to actually work in — or scaling stalls out halfway through, and your customers

407
00:29:48,115 --> 00:29:52,731
experience that half-finished scale-up as an outage indistinguishable from any other kind.

408
00:29:52,731 --> 00:29:55,038
Let's build the pod layer first.

409
00:29:55,038 --> 00:29:56,192
On screen: helm/templates/api-hpa.yaml.

410
00:29:56,192 --> 00:30:01,962
We set minReplicas to 3 — never scaling below a baseline that still respects our

411
00:30:01,962 --> 00:30:07,731
earlier AZ-spread and PodDisruptionBudget requirements, which we'll properly formalize in the very next lecture —

412
00:30:07,731 --> 00:30:13,500
and maxReplicas to 20, an explicit ceiling that exists for a specific, deliberate reason: without

413
00:30:13,500 --> 00:30:19,269
an upper bound, a sufficiently aggressive traffic spike, or worse, a runaway bug generating requests

414
00:30:19,269 --> 00:30:25,038
in an infinite loop, could scale your fleet essentially without limit, and your AWS bill

415
00:30:25,038 --> 00:30:26,192
along with it.

416
00:30:26,192 --> 00:30:31,962
A cap isn't a limitation you're grudgingly accepting — it's a guardrail you're deliberately choosing,

417
00:30:31,962 --> 00:30:37,731
converting "potentially unlimited cost" into "capacity ceiling I've consciously decided is my absolute worst case."

418
00:30:37,731 --> 00:30:41,962
Notice HPA is watching three separate signals simultaneously, not just one.

419
00:30:41,962 --> 00:30:44,269
CPU utilization, targeting 70 percent average.

420
00:30:44,269 --> 00:30:46,192
Memory utilization, targeting 80 percent.

421
00:30:46,192 --> 00:30:50,423
And a custom metric, requests per second, targeting 100 per pod.

422
00:30:50,423 --> 00:30:55,038
Why three, instead of picking the one that seems most obviously relevant?

423
00:30:55,038 --> 00:31:00,808
Because different kinds of load stress different resources, and a system watching only CPU can

424
00:31:00,808 --> 00:31:06,577
be caught completely flat-footed by a memory-bound spike, or by a request pattern that's cheap

425
00:31:06,577 --> 00:31:11,962
on compute but expensive in some other way HPA wasn't told to watch for.

426
00:31:11,962 --> 00:31:17,731
HPA takes the maximum recommended replica count across all configured metrics, so any one of

427
00:31:17,731 --> 00:31:23,500
the three tripping its threshold is enough to trigger scaling — the metrics work as

428
00:31:23,500 --> 00:31:29,269
independent, parallel tripwires, not as a single averaged signal that a spike in one dimension

429
00:31:29,269 --> 00:31:30,808
could get diluted by.

430
00:31:30,808 --> 00:31:36,577
The behavior block underneath is where a lot of the actual engineering judgment lives, and

431
00:31:36,577 --> 00:31:41,577
it's worth reading closely, because the asymmetry here is deliberate, not an oversight.

432
00:31:41,577 --> 00:31:47,346
Scaling up is configured to happen essentially instantly — a zero-second stabilization window, with policies

433
00:31:47,346 --> 00:31:53,115
allowing either doubling the current replica count or adding four pods every fifteen seconds, whichever

434
00:31:53,115 --> 00:31:54,654
adds more capacity faster.

435
00:31:54,654 --> 00:32:00,423
Scaling down, by sharp contrast, waits a full five-minute stabilization window before removing anything, and

436
00:32:00,423 --> 00:32:05,038
even then only removes at most 10 percent of replicas per minute.

437
00:32:05,038 --> 00:32:10,808
This asymmetry reflects a genuinely important, generally-true principle in reliability engineering: the cost of scaling

438
00:32:10,808 --> 00:32:16,577
up too slowly is customers experiencing real, immediate degradation, right now, while the cost of

439
00:32:16,577 --> 00:32:22,346
scaling down too slowly is a comparatively small amount of wasted compute spend for a

440
00:32:22,346 --> 00:32:23,500
few extra minutes.

441
00:32:23,500 --> 00:32:29,269
Given that asymmetric downside, react to growth in demand aggressively and immediately, and retreat from

442
00:32:29,269 --> 00:32:35,038
a passing spike cautiously and gradually — because a spike that looks over might just

443
00:32:35,038 --> 00:32:40,808
be a brief lull in the middle of a bigger one, and you'd rather find

444
00:32:40,808 --> 00:32:46,577
that out from slightly excess capacity sitting idle for a few minutes than from a

445
00:32:46,577 --> 00:32:51,577
second wave of traffic hitting a fleet you already prematurely scaled back down.

446
00:32:51,577 --> 00:32:57,346
Let's make that asymmetry concrete with real numbers, because the abstract principle is easy to

447
00:32:57,346 --> 00:33:00,808
nod along with and easy to configure wrong anyway.

448
00:33:00,808 --> 00:33:04,654
Say your baseline is three replicas, comfortably handling normal traffic.

449
00:33:04,654 --> 00:33:10,423
A spike hits, CPU utilization crosses 70 percent, and within fifteen seconds HPA can add

450
00:33:10,423 --> 00:33:16,192
up to four new pods, or double your current count, whichever is larger — so

451
00:33:16,192 --> 00:33:21,962
within fifteen seconds you could be at seven replicas, and within thirty seconds, if the

452
00:33:21,962 --> 00:33:25,038
spike is still climbing, considerably more than that.

453
00:33:25,038 --> 00:33:29,269
That's the "react immediately" half of the asymmetry, doing real work.

454
00:33:29,269 --> 00:33:35,038
Now the spike passes, load drops back to baseline, and utilization falls comfortably under 70

455
00:33:35,038 --> 00:33:35,808
percent again.

456
00:33:35,808 --> 00:33:41,577
Scaling down does not mirror that speed at all — it waits a full five

457
00:33:41,577 --> 00:33:47,346
minutes of sustained lower load before removing anything, and even then removes at most 10

458
00:33:47,346 --> 00:33:53,115
percent of current replicas per minute, a slow, deliberate crawl back down rather than the

459
00:33:53,115 --> 00:33:55,808
same immediate snap we saw scaling up.

460
00:33:55,808 --> 00:34:01,577
Fifteen seconds to add capacity, multiple minutes to remove it — and that lopsided ratio

461
00:34:01,577 --> 00:34:07,346
is the entire design philosophy of this behavior block, expressed in two numbers instead of

462
00:34:07,346 --> 00:34:08,885
one paragraph of explanation.

463
00:34:08,885 --> 00:34:14,654
Now for the layer underneath: Karpenter, watching for pods that HPA has created but that

464
00:34:14,654 --> 00:34:18,885
can't actually be scheduled anywhere, because no existing node has room.

465
00:34:18,885 --> 00:34:20,038
On screen: karpenter/nodepool.yaml.

466
00:34:20,038 --> 00:34:25,808
The requirements section is genuinely interesting, and it's where cost engineering and availability engineering turn

467
00:34:25,808 --> 00:34:30,038
out to be more compatible than people often assume going in.

468
00:34:30,038 --> 00:34:35,808
We allow both on-demand and spot instance types — spot instances cost meaningfully less, often

469
00:34:35,808 --> 00:34:41,577
somewhere in the range of 60 to 90 percent less than on-demand pricing, but AWS

470
00:34:41,577 --> 00:34:46,577
can reclaim them with only a short warning if capacity is needed elsewhere.

471
00:34:46,577 --> 00:34:52,346
For a stateless API layer that's already resilient to individual pod loss — remember, we

472
00:34:52,346 --> 00:34:58,115
built exactly that resilience with topology spread and PDBs — a spot instance disappearing is

473
00:34:58,115 --> 00:35:00,038
a shrug, not a crisis.

474
00:35:00,038 --> 00:35:05,808
Your other replicas, spread across other zones and other nodes, absorb the loss instantly, and

475
00:35:05,808 --> 00:35:07,731
Karpenter simply provisions a replacement.

476
00:35:07,731 --> 00:35:13,500
This is precisely the kind of workload spot instances were built for, and it's a

477
00:35:13,500 --> 00:35:19,269
genuinely good trade: meaningfully lower cost, in exchange for a risk your architecture has already

478
00:35:19,269 --> 00:35:21,962
been engineered to absorb without customer impact.

479
00:35:21,962 --> 00:35:27,731
The disruption block governs Karpenter's other job: not just adding nodes when needed, but consolidating

480
00:35:27,731 --> 00:35:30,038
them back down when they're not.

481
00:35:30,038 --> 00:35:35,808
consolidationPolicy set to WhenUnderutilized means Karpenter continuously looks for opportunities to replace several partially-empty, wastefully-provisioned

482
00:35:35,808 --> 00:35:41,577
nodes with fewer, more tightly-packed ones — actively fighting the kind of slow, silent cost

483
00:35:41,577 --> 00:35:47,346
creep that happens when a cluster scales up under load and simply never scales back

484
00:35:47,346 --> 00:35:48,500
down cleanly afterward.

485
00:35:48,500 --> 00:35:54,269
We also set expireAfter to 720 hours, thirty days — forcing nodes to cycle out

486
00:35:54,269 --> 00:36:00,038
periodically even if nothing else prompts it, which ensures nodes are periodically replaced with fresh

487
00:36:00,038 --> 00:36:05,808
images carrying the latest security patches, rather than quietly aging for a year, accumulating unpatched

488
00:36:05,808 --> 00:36:07,346
vulnerabilities the whole time.

489
00:36:07,346 --> 00:36:10,423
Let's actually watch this work under real load.

490
00:36:10,423 --> 00:36:16,192
On screen: a load test using a tool called hey, generating sustained concurrent traffic against

491
00:36:16,192 --> 00:36:18,115
our API for five minutes.

492
00:36:18,115 --> 00:36:23,885
We tail kubectl get hpa with the watch flag, observing the current replica count climb

493
00:36:23,885 --> 00:36:28,500
in real time as CPU and request-rate metrics cross their configured thresholds.

494
00:36:28,500 --> 00:36:34,269
Simultaneously, we tail Karpenter's own controller logs, watching a specific, satisfying sequence unfold: a burst

495
00:36:34,269 --> 00:36:40,038
of newly-pending pods triggers a scale-up decision, Karpenter provisions new nodes to make room, and

496
00:36:40,038 --> 00:36:44,654
moments later those pending pods get successfully scheduled onto the freshly-provisioned capacity.

497
00:36:44,654 --> 00:36:50,423
Three layers — HPA deciding more pods are needed, Karpenter deciding more room is needed,

498
00:36:50,423 --> 00:36:56,192
the scheduler placing pods onto that new room — working in a coordinated sequence that,

499
00:36:56,192 --> 00:37:01,962
done right, an actual user experiences as nothing more than the site staying responsive throughout

500
00:37:01,962 --> 00:37:07,731
a traffic spike they never even knew was happening on the other side of the

501
00:37:07,731 --> 00:37:08,431
screen.

502
00:37:08,431 --> 00:37:09,585
Here's your checkpoint.

503
00:37:09,585 --> 00:37:15,354
Run a sustained load test against your API and watch HPA scale replicas upward in

504
00:37:15,354 --> 00:37:18,046
response to real metrics, not synthetic ones.

505
00:37:18,046 --> 00:37:21,123
Watch Karpenter provision new nodes to accommodate them.

506
00:37:21,123 --> 00:37:26,892
Once the load test ends, keep watching — confirm the system actually scales back down

507
00:37:26,892 --> 00:37:32,662
afterward, on its own, rather than staying pinned at peak capacity, quietly burning money for

508
00:37:32,662 --> 00:37:34,585
a spike that already ended.

509
00:37:34,585 --> 00:37:40,354
Time the whole cycle, from the first sign of increased load to new pods genuinely

510
00:37:40,354 --> 00:37:46,123
serving live traffic, and separately, from load ending to the fleet settling back to baseline.

511
00:37:46,123 --> 00:37:51,892
Both numbers matter, and for very different reasons — one tells your auditor how fast

512
00:37:51,892 --> 00:37:57,662
you respond to a real spike, the other tells your finance team how efficiently you

513
00:37:57,662 --> 00:38:01,892
stop paying for capacity the moment you no longer need it.

514
00:38:01,892 --> 00:38:07,662
Financial RAG's traffic spikes are user-driven — a research note goes out, everyone's suddenly asking

515
00:38:07,662 --> 00:38:09,200
about the same ticker.

516
00:38:09,200 --> 00:38:14,969
RiskOracle's spikes are calendar-driven instead: end of day, end of quarter, when every portfolio's risk

517
00:38:14,969 --> 00:38:20,738
gets recalculated at once, in a tight window, whether or not any human is actively

518
00:38:20,738 --> 00:38:21,438
watching.

519
00:38:21,438 --> 00:38:25,669
That's a meaningfully different load pattern, worth configuring HPA differently for.

520
00:38:25,669 --> 00:38:29,515
A user-driven spike justifies reacting fast and scaling down cautiously.

521
00:38:29,515 --> 00:38:35,285
A calendar-driven spike you can actually predict — RiskOracle can scale up proactively a few

522
00:38:35,285 --> 00:38:41,054
minutes before the known daily batch starts, rather than reacting to CPU metrics after the

523
00:38:41,054 --> 00:38:41,754
fact.

524
00:38:41,754 --> 00:38:47,523
Same HPA and Karpenter mechanics, genuinely different tuning, because the two systems are being stressed

525
00:38:47,523 --> 00:38:50,215
by two different kinds of real-world pressure.

526
00:38:50,215 --> 00:38:55,985
Here's a genuinely uncomfortable statistic worth sitting with before we go any further: a meaningful

527
00:38:55,985 --> 00:39:01,754
share of real-world "availability incidents" that companies report to auditors weren't caused by an attacker,

528
00:39:01,754 --> 00:39:07,523
weren't caused by a hardware failure, and weren't caused by a traffic spike at all.

529
00:39:07,523 --> 00:39:10,985
They were caused by the company's own maintenance work.

530
00:39:10,985 --> 00:39:12,908
A routine Kubernetes version upgrade.

531
00:39:12,908 --> 00:39:15,985
A node getting patched for a security update.

532
00:39:15,985 --> 00:39:21,754
Someone on the infrastructure team doing exactly what they were supposed to be doing —

533
00:39:21,754 --> 00:39:27,523
and, in doing it, accidentally taking down every single replica of a critical service at

534
00:39:27,523 --> 00:39:33,292
once, because nothing in the system's configuration told Kubernetes "don't ever do that." This is

535
00:39:33,292 --> 00:39:39,062
precisely the gap PodDisruptionBudgets exist to close, and it's worth being crisp about a distinction

536
00:39:39,062 --> 00:39:44,831
most engineers blur together the first time they encounter it: Kubernetes has always distinguished between

537
00:39:44,831 --> 00:39:50,600
involuntary disruptions — a node hardware failure, an out-of-memory kill, the unpredictable stuff you genuinely

538
00:39:50,600 --> 00:39:56,369
cannot schedule around — and voluntary disruptions — a node drain for maintenance, a rolling

539
00:39:56,369 --> 00:39:59,446
deployment, a manual scale-down someone chose to trigger.

540
00:39:59,446 --> 00:40:05,215
Nothing in this phase can eliminate involuntary disruptions; that's what everything from Multi-AZ through Karpenter

541
00:40:05,215 --> 00:40:07,138
is already busy defending against.

542
00:40:07,138 --> 00:40:11,369
But voluntary disruptions are, definitionally, choices your own team is making.

543
00:40:11,369 --> 00:40:17,138
A PodDisruptionBudget is how you tell Kubernetes: "you're allowed to evict pods from this deployment

544
00:40:17,138 --> 00:40:22,908
for routine maintenance, but never past this specific line — even if it means the

545
00:40:22,908 --> 00:40:28,677
maintenance operation itself has to wait." Let's build these for every stateful and critical component

546
00:40:28,677 --> 00:40:34,446
in our system, because a PDB on your API but nowhere else is exactly the

547
00:40:34,446 --> 00:40:40,215
kind of half-finished protection we warned about back in Lecture 6.1's common-mistakes aside — solving

548
00:40:40,215 --> 00:40:45,985
the visible, obvious case while quietly leaving an identical gap sitting right next to it.

549
00:40:45,985 --> 00:40:47,138
On screen: helm/templates/api-pdb.yaml.

550
00:40:47,138 --> 00:40:49,831
minAvailable set to 2 for our API.

551
00:40:49,831 --> 00:40:55,600
This is a floor, not a target — it says at least two replicas must

552
00:40:55,600 --> 00:41:01,369
remain healthy and running at all times, no matter how many total replicas currently exist

553
00:41:01,369 --> 00:41:04,831
or how urgently a node needs draining for maintenance.

554
00:41:04,831 --> 00:41:10,600
For our agent deployment, we instead use maxUnavailable set to 1 — a ceiling rather

555
00:41:10,600 --> 00:41:16,369
than a floor, saying at most one replica may be disrupted at any given moment.

556
00:41:16,369 --> 00:41:22,138
These two phrasings — minAvailable and maxUnavailable — are mathematically related but not identical, and

557
00:41:22,138 --> 00:41:27,908
choosing between them is a genuine design decision worth making deliberately rather than by habit.

558
00:41:27,908 --> 00:41:33,677
minAvailable is the more intuitive choice when you have a hard floor you never want

559
00:41:33,677 --> 00:41:38,677
crossed, regardless of how the deployment's total replica count might change over time.

560
00:41:38,677 --> 00:41:44,446
maxUnavailable is often more natural for a fleet where the replica count itself scales up

561
00:41:44,446 --> 00:41:50,215
and down — like our HPA-scaled API and agent deployments — since it automatically expresses

562
00:41:50,215 --> 00:41:55,985
"one at a time" whether your current fleet size is three replicas or twenty, without

563
00:41:55,985 --> 00:42:00,215
needing to be manually recalculated every time HPA changes the total.

564
00:42:00,215 --> 00:42:05,985
For our stateful components — the pgvector database pod and Redis, both built as StatefulSets

565
00:42:05,985 --> 00:42:11,754
specifically because of the storage-and-identity requirements we discussed back in Lecture 6.2 — we set

566
00:42:11,754 --> 00:42:12,908
minAvailable to 1.

567
00:42:12,908 --> 00:42:18,677
This deserves honest, specific attention, because it exposes a genuine limitation baked into how we've

568
00:42:18,677 --> 00:42:23,677
configured Redis so far in this course, not a flaw in PDBs themselves.

569
00:42:23,677 --> 00:42:27,138
Recall that our Redis StatefulSet runs a single replica.

570
00:42:27,138 --> 00:42:32,908
A PDB requiring "at least 1 available" on a single-replica deployment doesn't actually prevent disruption

571
00:42:32,908 --> 00:42:38,677
at all — it just means Kubernetes will refuse to voluntarily evict that one and

572
00:42:38,677 --> 00:42:44,446
only pod unless a legitimate replacement can be scheduled first, which, for a genuine node

573
00:42:44,446 --> 00:42:50,215
drain, buys you very little real protection, since there's no second replica standing by to

574
00:42:50,215 --> 00:42:52,908
absorb traffic while the first one moves.

575
00:42:52,908 --> 00:42:58,677
A PDB is a coordination mechanism between multiple replicas — it has real teeth on

576
00:42:58,677 --> 00:43:04,446
our three-replica pgvector StatefulSet, but it's closer to a formality than a genuine safety net

577
00:43:04,446 --> 00:43:10,215
on our single-replica Redis, and it's worth being honest with yourself, and eventually with your

578
00:43:10,215 --> 00:43:15,215
auditor, about exactly where that real protection begins and where it's mostly symbolic.

579
00:43:15,215 --> 00:43:18,292
Let's watch a PDB actually do its job.

580
00:43:18,292 --> 00:43:24,062
On screen: kubectl drain, targeting a node currently hosting API pods, run first with the

581
00:43:24,062 --> 00:43:29,831
dry-run flag so we can see what would happen without actually forcing anything to occur.

582
00:43:29,831 --> 00:43:35,600
If draining that particular node would push our healthy API replica count below the minAvailable

583
00:43:35,600 --> 00:43:41,369
threshold of 2, the drain command simply refuses, returning a clear, specific message naming the

584
00:43:41,369 --> 00:43:43,292
exact PodDisruptionBudget that's blocking it.

585
00:43:43,292 --> 00:43:49,062
This is, genuinely, the system working as intended — not a bug, not an inconvenience,

586
00:43:49,062 --> 00:43:54,831
but Kubernetes actively protecting your stated availability commitment from your own team's routine maintenance work,

587
00:43:54,831 --> 00:43:57,908
exactly the way a good safety interlock should.

588
00:43:57,908 --> 00:44:03,677
Notice there's also a way to force the issue anyway — kubectl drain with the

589
00:44:03,677 --> 00:44:09,446
disable-eviction flag — and it's worth being explicit about why we're showing it to you

590
00:44:09,446 --> 00:44:15,215
at all: not as a recommended technique, but as a documented escape hatch that exists

591
00:44:15,215 --> 00:44:18,677
precisely because emergencies sometimes require overriding normal safety rails.

592
00:44:18,677 --> 00:44:24,446
If you ever find yourself reaching for that flag in production, it should be a

593
00:44:24,446 --> 00:44:30,215
deliberate, documented, consciously-made exception — not a habit that quietly becomes how your team handles

594
00:44:30,215 --> 00:44:35,985
every inconvenient PDB block, which would defeat the entire purpose of having configured one in

595
00:44:35,985 --> 00:44:37,138
the first place.

596
00:44:37,138 --> 00:44:42,908
We also wire up Prometheus alerting specifically for PDB-related events — watching for cases where

597
00:44:42,908 --> 00:44:48,677
the current number of healthy pods for a given PDB drops below its desired healthy

598
00:44:48,677 --> 00:44:50,600
count, sustained for five minutes.

599
00:44:50,600 --> 00:44:56,369
This matters because a PDB silently blocking a drain isn't inherently a crisis on its

600
00:44:56,369 --> 00:45:02,138
own — it might just mean maintenance has to wait a few minutes for HPA

601
00:45:02,138 --> 00:45:07,908
to spin up one more replica first — but if it's happening constantly, or blocking

602
00:45:07,908 --> 00:45:13,677
indefinitely, that's a genuine signal that your baseline replica counts and your maintenance cadence have

603
00:45:13,677 --> 00:45:19,446
quietly drifted out of alignment with each other, and a human needs to go look

604
00:45:19,446 --> 00:45:20,215
at why.

605
00:45:20,215 --> 00:45:21,369
Here's your checkpoint.

606
00:45:21,369 --> 00:45:27,138
Set up PodDisruptionBudgets for every critical component — API, agent workers, pgvector, and Redis —

607
00:45:27,138 --> 00:45:32,908
being honest with yourself, as we just discussed, about how much real protection each one

608
00:45:32,908 --> 00:45:36,754
is actually providing given the underlying replica count it's protecting.

609
00:45:36,754 --> 00:45:42,523
Simulate a node failure by cordoning and draining a node hosting API pods, and confirm

610
00:45:42,523 --> 00:45:48,292
the PDB correctly blocks the drain if — and only if — it would violate

611
00:45:48,292 --> 00:45:49,446
your configured minimum.

612
00:45:49,446 --> 00:45:55,215
Then verify the opposite case too, which people often skip: confirm that when you have

613
00:45:55,215 --> 00:46:00,985
genuine spare replica capacity, the exact same drain command succeeds cleanly, without needing the force

614
00:46:00,985 --> 00:46:02,138
flag at all.

615
00:46:02,138 --> 00:46:07,908
A safety rail that blocks every single operation indiscriminately isn't actually protecting availability — it's

616
00:46:07,908 --> 00:46:13,677
just gotten in everyone's way so consistently that your team has learned to route around

617
00:46:13,677 --> 00:46:18,677
it entirely, which is arguably worse than not having the rail at all.

618
00:46:18,677 --> 00:46:22,908
Financial RAG's PodDisruptionBudget protects its API and its single pgvector instance.

619
00:46:22,908 --> 00:46:28,292
RiskOracle needs its own, separately configured, and here's the detail worth being honest about.

620
00:46:28,292 --> 00:46:34,062
If RiskOracle's risk-scoring service happens to run with only two replicas because it's comparatively expensive

621
00:46:34,062 --> 00:46:39,831
to run, a PDB requiring one available replica minimum is providing real but genuinely thin

622
00:46:39,831 --> 00:46:44,831
protection, the same honest limitation we just discussed for our own single-replica Redis.

623
00:46:44,831 --> 00:46:50,215
Check RiskOracle's actual replica count before you assume its PDB is doing meaningful work.

624
00:46:50,215 --> 00:46:55,985
A PDB wrapped around a two-replica service is a real control, but it's a noticeably

625
00:46:55,985 --> 00:47:01,754
weaker one than the same PDB wrapped around six replicas, and your evidence should say

626
00:47:01,754 --> 00:47:04,062
so honestly rather than implying otherwise.

627
00:47:04,062 --> 00:47:09,831
Before we move to the database's read side, let's pause for four questions that come

628
00:47:09,831 --> 00:47:15,600
up constantly at exactly this point in the course, and answer each one precisely rather

629
00:47:15,600 --> 00:47:17,138
than hand-waving past it.

630
00:47:17,138 --> 00:47:22,138
Doesn't running spot instances through Karpenter contradict everything we just built for availability?

631
00:47:22,138 --> 00:47:27,523
Good instinct, and the answer is no, specifically because of what we already built.

632
00:47:27,523 --> 00:47:32,908
Topology spread constraints keep any single instance from holding a disproportionate share of capacity.

633
00:47:32,908 --> 00:47:37,523
PodDisruptionBudgets keep any single loss from dropping you below your safe floor.

634
00:47:37,523 --> 00:47:40,215
Karpenter replaces a reclaimed spot instance automatically.

635
00:47:40,215 --> 00:47:45,985
Spot instances are a reasonable trade precisely because the surrounding resilience layer already makes losing

636
00:47:45,985 --> 00:47:47,138
one a non-event.

637
00:47:47,138 --> 00:47:52,908
What's the actual difference between Multi-AZ failover and our quarterly restore test, since they sound

638
00:47:52,908 --> 00:47:55,215
like they solve the same problem?

639
00:47:55,215 --> 00:47:55,985
They don't.

640
00:47:55,985 --> 00:48:01,754
Multi-AZ handles infrastructure failure, a server or data center physically breaking, by switching to an

641
00:48:01,754 --> 00:48:05,215
already-synchronized standby in minutes with essentially no data loss.

642
00:48:05,215 --> 00:48:10,985
It does nothing for a human mistake, a DELETE without a WHERE clause, or slow

643
00:48:10,985 --> 00:48:11,754
data corruption.

644
00:48:11,754 --> 00:48:17,523
That's what point-in-time restore exists for instead: recovering to a moment before the mistake, on

645
00:48:17,523 --> 00:48:20,985
a timescale measured in tens of minutes, not seconds.

646
00:48:20,985 --> 00:48:22,523
You genuinely need both.

647
00:48:22,523 --> 00:48:27,523
Our PDB blocked a node drain during an actual maintenance window last week.

648
00:48:27,523 --> 00:48:29,062
Was it right to?

649
00:48:29,062 --> 00:48:34,831
Check first: were you already at your configured minimum for some other reason, an earlier

650
00:48:34,831 --> 00:48:37,908
unrelated node failure that hadn't been replaced yet?

651
00:48:37,908 --> 00:48:40,985
If so, the PDB did exactly its job.

652
00:48:40,985 --> 00:48:46,754
If you had genuine spare capacity sitting idle, that's worth investigating as a possible misconfiguration,

653
00:48:46,754 --> 00:48:51,369
not a reason to reach for the force-drain flag out of habit.

654
00:48:51,369 --> 00:48:57,138
How do we actually decide our SLO target, or is ninety-nine point nine percent just

655
00:48:57,138 --> 00:48:58,677
the default everyone copies?

656
00:48:58,677 --> 00:49:00,985
It should be specific to you.

657
00:49:00,985 --> 00:49:03,677
Check what your customers' contracts actually require.

658
00:49:03,677 --> 00:49:06,754
Check honestly what your current architecture can support.

659
00:49:06,754 --> 00:49:12,523
And respect the real cost curve: each additional nine costs disproportionately more than the one

660
00:49:12,523 --> 00:49:13,292
before it.

661
00:49:13,292 --> 00:49:19,062
Pick a number you can genuinely defend with real evidence, not one that simply sounds

662
00:49:19,062 --> 00:49:20,985
impressive on a sales page.

663
00:49:20,985 --> 00:49:26,754
With those questions answered, let's talk about a failure mode that doesn't announce itself the

664
00:49:26,754 --> 00:49:32,523
way a crashed pod or a downed AZ does — it doesn't show up as

665
00:49:32,523 --> 00:49:34,062
a dramatic red alert.

666
00:49:34,062 --> 00:49:39,831
It shows up as everything just getting slower, and slower, and slower, until one day

667
00:49:39,831 --> 00:49:45,600
it crosses an invisible line and your availability SLO is blown, without a single obvious,

668
00:49:45,600 --> 00:49:51,369
discrete event you could point to as "the moment it broke." Here's how it happens.

669
00:49:51,369 --> 00:49:57,138
Your primary database is handling both jobs at once: every write your application makes, and

670
00:49:57,138 --> 00:50:02,523
every single read too — every ticker lookup, every history query, every dashboard render.

671
00:50:02,523 --> 00:50:07,138
Writes are relatively rare and cheap, if your table design is sound.

672
00:50:07,138 --> 00:50:12,908
Reads, in a system like ours, are usually the overwhelming majority of total database traffic,

673
00:50:12,908 --> 00:50:15,985
often by an order of magnitude or more.

674
00:50:15,985 --> 00:50:21,754
As your user base grows, read volume grows right alongside it, and eventually your primary

675
00:50:21,754 --> 00:50:27,523
database is spending the vast majority of its capacity just answering questions, leaving less and

676
00:50:27,523 --> 00:50:33,292
less headroom for the writes that actually matter most for your data's integrity — the

677
00:50:33,292 --> 00:50:37,523
exact writes we spent an entire earlier phase making cryptographically bulletproof.

678
00:50:37,523 --> 00:50:43,292
A read replica is the fix, and the underlying idea is genuinely simple, even if

679
00:50:43,292 --> 00:50:49,062
the engineering has some real subtlety hiding underneath it: create one or more additional database

680
00:50:49,062 --> 00:50:54,831
instances that continuously receive a copy of every change made to the primary, and route

681
00:50:54,831 --> 00:51:00,600
your read-only queries to those replicas instead, freeing the primary to focus overwhelmingly on writes.

682
00:51:00,600 --> 00:51:06,369
Here's the critical distinction worth understanding clearly, especially coming right after Multi-AZ, because the two

683
00:51:06,369 --> 00:51:09,446
mechanisms sound similar but serve genuinely different purposes.

684
00:51:09,446 --> 00:51:15,215
Multi-AZ, from Lecture 6.1, uses synchronous replication specifically for failover readiness — the standby is

685
00:51:15,215 --> 00:51:20,985
never allowed to be behind, precisely because it might need to become the primary at

686
00:51:20,985 --> 00:51:22,908
any given moment, without warning.

687
00:51:22,908 --> 00:51:28,677
Read replicas use asynchronous replication instead, optimized for scaling read throughput rather than for instant

688
00:51:28,677 --> 00:51:34,446
failover — meaning a read replica can lag behind the primary by some measurable amount,

689
00:51:34,446 --> 00:51:37,138
typically milliseconds, occasionally longer under heavy load.

690
00:51:37,138 --> 00:51:40,600
That lag isn't a defect somebody forgot to fix.

691
00:51:40,600 --> 00:51:46,369
It's the direct, unavoidable consequence of the specific trade-off being made: asynchronous replication lets the

692
00:51:46,369 --> 00:51:52,138
primary acknowledge a write instantly, without needing to wait for a replica sitting in possibly

693
00:51:52,138 --> 00:51:57,908
a different Availability Zone to confirm receipt first, which is exactly the design choice that

694
00:51:57,908 --> 00:52:01,754
makes replicas practical at meaningful scale in the first place.

695
00:52:01,754 --> 00:52:07,523
That lag has a real, occasionally surprising consequence worth internalizing before you build anything on

696
00:52:07,523 --> 00:52:13,292
top of it: a user could, in principle, write new data through your API, and

697
00:52:13,292 --> 00:52:19,062
then immediately re-query it and briefly not see their own update yet, if that particular

698
00:52:19,062 --> 00:52:23,677
read happens to land on a replica that hasn't quite caught up.

699
00:52:23,677 --> 00:52:29,446
This is exactly why read-your-own-writes consistency is a genuine architectural decision, not an accident to

700
00:52:29,446 --> 00:52:35,215
stumble into — for any workflow where a user needs to see their own change

701
00:52:35,215 --> 00:52:40,985
reflected immediately, you deliberately route that specific follow-up read to the primary rather than to

702
00:52:40,985 --> 00:52:46,754
a replica, accepting slightly less scaling benefit for that one narrow case in exchange for

703
00:52:46,754 --> 00:52:52,523
a UI that doesn't briefly, confusingly appear to have silently dropped someone's own recent action.

704
00:52:52,523 --> 00:52:53,677
Let's build it.

705
00:52:53,677 --> 00:52:54,831
On screen: terraform/modules/rds/read-replica.tf.

706
00:52:54,831 --> 00:53:00,600
We provision two replicas for production, each one explicitly pinned to a specific Availability Zone

707
00:53:00,600 --> 00:53:06,369
via count.index, spreading them intentionally rather than leaving placement to chance — the same underlying

708
00:53:06,369 --> 00:53:11,754
AZ-diversity principle from Lecture 6.1, applied here to read capacity instead of write capacity.

709
00:53:11,754 --> 00:53:15,600
Notice backup_retention_period is explicitly set to zero on the replicas.

710
00:53:15,600 --> 00:53:21,369
Backups on a replica would be redundant work, and genuinely a little bit of a

711
00:53:21,369 --> 00:53:27,138
category error — a replica's data is, by definition, already a live, continuously-updating copy of

712
00:53:27,138 --> 00:53:32,908
the primary, and the primary itself is already where our actual backup strategy, and its

713
00:53:32,908 --> 00:53:35,600
own thirty-day retention, correctly and deliberately lives.

714
00:53:35,600 --> 00:53:38,292
Now let's wire this into the application.

715
00:53:38,292 --> 00:53:40,985
On screen: src/financial_rag/storage/database.py, a class called DatabaseClient.

716
00:53:40,985 --> 00:53:46,754
It maintains a single write_engine, pointed permanently at the primary, and a list of read_engines,

717
00:53:46,754 --> 00:53:48,677
one connection pool per replica.

718
00:53:48,677 --> 00:53:54,446
The method get_write_session always routes to the primary — no exceptions, no cleverness, every INSERT,

719
00:53:54,446 --> 00:53:57,138
UPDATE, and DELETE goes there, full stop.

720
00:53:57,138 --> 00:54:02,908
get_read_session, by contrast, distributes across the available replicas using simple round-robin — cycling through them

721
00:54:02,908 --> 00:54:08,677
in strict sequence — spreading read load roughly evenly rather than hammering whichever replica happens

722
00:54:08,677 --> 00:54:10,985
to be first in a list.

723
00:54:10,985 --> 00:54:16,754
There's also a get_random_read_session variant using random selection instead of strict round-robin, worth knowing about

724
00:54:16,754 --> 00:54:22,523
specifically because round-robin, in a system with many concurrent, independently-running application instances, can sometimes accidentally

725
00:54:22,523 --> 00:54:28,292
synchronize into an uneven pattern purely by unlucky timing coincidence, in a way pure randomness

726
00:54:28,292 --> 00:54:32,908
tends to avoid more reliably over a large enough sample of requests.

727
00:54:32,908 --> 00:54:38,677
Down in the repository layer, the pattern becomes genuinely simple to apply consistently: get_analysis_by_ticker, a

728
00:54:38,677 --> 00:54:40,215
read-only lookup, uses get_read_session.

729
00:54:40,215 --> 00:54:43,292
record_analysis, which performs an actual write, uses get_write_session.

730
00:54:43,292 --> 00:54:49,062
This distinction has to be made deliberately and consistently, method by method, throughout your entire

731
00:54:49,062 --> 00:54:54,831
codebase — there's no automatic mechanism forcing correctness here, which means a single careless line

732
00:54:54,831 --> 00:55:00,600
of code routing a write operation to a read replica connection will fail loudly and

733
00:55:00,600 --> 00:55:06,369
immediately, since replicas are configured read-only at the database engine level and simply reject write

734
00:55:06,369 --> 00:55:12,138
attempts outright — a genuinely reassuring fail-safe, much better than the alternative of a write

735
00:55:12,138 --> 00:55:17,908
attempt silently succeeding against the wrong target and quietly, invisibly diverging from your actual source

736
00:55:17,908 --> 00:55:18,677
of truth.

737
00:55:18,677 --> 00:55:24,446
Because replication lag is real and can genuinely vary under load, monitoring it isn't optional

738
00:55:24,446 --> 00:55:30,215
— it's how you'd actually notice if the gap between primary and replica quietly started

739
00:55:30,215 --> 00:55:33,677
growing beyond what's healthy for your specific use case.

740
00:55:33,677 --> 00:55:39,446
On screen: a direct SQL query against pg_stat_replication, showing each connected replica's sync state and,

741
00:55:39,446 --> 00:55:45,215
critically, pg_wal_lsn_diff — the write-ahead-log byte distance between what the primary has committed and what

742
00:55:45,215 --> 00:55:49,831
each specific replica has actually caught up to and replayed so far.

743
00:55:49,831 --> 00:55:55,600
We also expose this as a proper Prometheus metric, pg_replication_lag_seconds, letting you graph it over

744
00:55:55,600 --> 00:56:01,369
time and alert automatically if lag ever creeps past a threshold that would actually start

745
00:56:01,369 --> 00:56:04,446
to matter for your application's specific consistency needs.

746
00:56:04,446 --> 00:56:05,600
Here's your checkpoint.

747
00:56:05,600 --> 00:56:11,369
Provision read replicas for your RDS instance and update your repository layer so that reads

748
00:56:11,369 --> 00:56:17,138
and writes are routed to the correct destination — consistently, deliberately, method by method, not

749
00:56:17,138 --> 00:56:19,062
by accident or convention alone.

750
00:56:19,062 --> 00:56:24,831
Confirm, using actual database logs, that your SELECT traffic really is landing on the replicas

751
00:56:24,831 --> 00:56:26,369
rather than the primary.

752
00:56:26,369 --> 00:56:32,138
And specifically, deliberately test the read-your-own-writes scenario we discussed: write a record, then immediately try

753
00:56:32,138 --> 00:56:37,908
to read it back through a path that would normally hit a replica, and observe

754
00:56:37,908 --> 00:56:41,754
directly whether you can reproduce that brief, disorienting lag firsthand.

755
00:56:41,754 --> 00:56:46,754
Understanding a limitation by reading about it in a lecture is one thing.

756
00:56:46,754 --> 00:56:52,523
Watching it happen on your own screen, to data you just wrote yourself sixty milliseconds

757
00:56:52,523 --> 00:56:58,292
ago, tends to make the underlying trade-off click in a way no explanation alone quite

758
00:56:58,292 --> 00:56:58,992
manages.

759
00:56:58,992 --> 00:57:04,762
RiskOracle is actually the more compelling case for read replicas of the two systems, worth

760
00:57:04,762 --> 00:57:05,915
walking through directly.

761
00:57:05,915 --> 00:57:11,685
Its dashboards run heavy, aggregate queries, portfolio-wide risk summaries, historical trend charts, that can genuinely

762
00:57:11,685 --> 00:57:15,146
hammer a primary database if they land there directly.

763
00:57:15,146 --> 00:57:20,915
Route RiskOracle's dashboard queries to a read replica, keep its actual risk calculation writes on

764
00:57:20,915 --> 00:57:26,685
the primary, and a slow analyst dashboard query can no longer compete with, or slow

765
00:57:26,685 --> 00:57:30,531
down, the actual risk engine writing fresh calculations underneath it.

766
00:57:30,531 --> 00:57:36,300
Financial RAG benefits from this pattern too, but RiskOracle's read-heavy reporting workload is where the

767
00:57:36,300 --> 00:57:38,608
trade-off earns its keep most clearly.

768
00:57:38,608 --> 00:57:42,454
Build it there first if you're deciding where to start.

769
00:57:42,454 --> 00:57:48,223
Let me tell you about a pattern that shows up constantly across real post-incident reviews,

770
00:57:48,223 --> 00:57:51,300
the kind shared, slightly anonymized, at infrastructure conferences.

771
00:57:51,300 --> 00:57:57,069
A company builds exactly what we've built across this phase so far — Multi-AZ, autoscaling,

772
00:57:57,069 --> 00:58:00,915
PDBs, read replicas, all of it, carefully and correctly configured.

773
00:58:00,915 --> 00:58:03,608
Their architecture diagram is genuinely, legitimately excellent.

774
00:58:03,608 --> 00:58:09,377
Then, eighteen months later, a real AZ outage actually happens — not a drill, the

775
00:58:09,377 --> 00:58:15,146
genuine article — and instead of the clean, seconds-long failover the documentation promised, the system

776
00:58:15,146 --> 00:58:17,454
stays degraded for nearly forty minutes.

777
00:58:17,454 --> 00:58:20,146
Nothing in the architecture was wrong, exactly.

778
00:58:20,146 --> 00:58:25,915
It was something nobody had thought to test directly: a piece of internal tooling, written

779
00:58:25,915 --> 00:58:31,685
eight months after the original architecture was designed, that happened to hard-code one specific database

780
00:58:31,685 --> 00:58:37,454
endpoint rather than using the properly load-balanced, failover-aware connection string everyone else in the system

781
00:58:37,454 --> 00:58:38,223
correctly used.

782
00:58:38,223 --> 00:58:43,992
It had worked flawlessly, silently, for eight uneventful months, precisely because nothing had ever actually

783
00:58:43,992 --> 00:58:47,454
forced a real failover to occur and expose it.

784
00:58:47,454 --> 00:58:50,146
That's not a story about bad engineering.

785
00:58:50,146 --> 00:58:55,915
It's a story about the specific, sneaky gap between "we built resilient architecture" and "we

786
00:58:55,915 --> 00:59:01,685
have actually, recently, personally watched that architecture survive a real failure." Every piece of tooling,

787
00:59:01,685 --> 00:59:07,454
every script, every internal dashboard that gets built after your original resilience work is finished

788
00:59:07,454 --> 00:59:13,223
is a fresh opportunity for exactly this kind of quiet, invisible assumption to creep back

789
00:59:13,223 --> 00:59:18,992
in, one hard-coded connection string at a time, completely unnoticed until the one day it

790
00:59:18,992 --> 00:59:19,762
genuinely matters.

791
00:59:19,762 --> 00:59:25,531
Let's pause here, because we've now built five genuinely serious layers of resilience — Multi-AZ

792
00:59:25,531 --> 00:59:31,300
everywhere, persistent Redis, two-layer autoscaling, PodDisruptionBudgets, read replicas — and it would be completely reasonable

793
00:59:31,300 --> 00:59:35,915
at this point to feel like the hard work is essentially done.

794
00:59:35,915 --> 00:59:41,685
Here's the uncomfortable question worth asking honestly before we move forward: how do you actually

795
00:59:41,685 --> 00:59:43,608
know any of it works?

796
00:59:43,608 --> 00:59:49,377
Not "how confident are you," not "does the configuration look correct on paper" — how

797
00:59:49,377 --> 00:59:53,608
do you know, with real, demonstrated evidence, rather than architectural optimism?

798
00:59:53,608 --> 00:59:59,377
Every fire department in the world has an answer to this exact question, and it's

799
00:59:59,377 --> 01:00:00,531
not a document.

800
01:00:00,531 --> 01:00:01,685
It's a drill.

801
01:00:01,685 --> 01:00:07,454
You don't discover whether an evacuation plan actually works by reading it closely and nodding

802
01:00:07,454 --> 01:00:08,223
along thoughtfully.

803
01:00:08,223 --> 01:00:13,992
You discover it by pulling the alarm on a random Tuesday afternoon when nobody's expecting

804
01:00:13,992 --> 01:00:19,762
it, and watching, with a stopwatch, what actually happens — which stairwells get congested, which

805
01:00:19,762 --> 01:00:25,531
exits people forget exist, which assumptions on paper turn out to be quietly wrong in

806
01:00:25,531 --> 01:00:29,762
practice, the moment reality gets involved instead of just the plan.

807
01:00:29,762 --> 01:00:35,531
Chaos engineering is that same fire drill, applied to distributed systems, and it rests on

808
01:00:35,531 --> 01:00:41,300
a genuinely important, slightly uncomfortable insight: the only way to know your system tolerates failure

809
01:00:41,300 --> 01:00:47,069
is to inject real failure into it, deliberately, ideally on your own schedule rather than

810
01:00:47,069 --> 01:00:52,838
waiting for reality to pick the timing for you at 3 AM on a night

811
01:00:52,838 --> 01:00:54,762
you're least prepared for it.

812
01:00:54,762 --> 01:01:00,531
LitmusChaos is the specific tool we'll use to run these controlled fire drills against our

813
01:01:00,531 --> 01:01:01,300
Kubernetes cluster.

814
01:01:01,300 --> 01:01:05,146
On screen: our first, simplest experiment, pod-delete, defined in chaos/pod-delete.yaml.

815
01:01:05,146 --> 01:01:10,915
It targets our API deployment specifically and deletes 50 percent of its running pods, repeating

816
01:01:10,915 --> 01:01:15,531
that deletion every ten seconds for a total window of sixty seconds.

817
01:01:15,531 --> 01:01:21,300
Think carefully for a moment about exactly what this is actually testing, because it's easy

818
01:01:21,300 --> 01:01:25,146
to conflate with something else it's not testing at all.

819
01:01:25,146 --> 01:01:30,915
This experiment isn't primarily testing HPA's ability to add capacity under genuine load — that's

820
01:01:30,915 --> 01:01:33,992
what Lecture 6.3's load test already specifically covered.

821
01:01:33,992 --> 01:01:39,762
It's testing something more foundational and, in some ways, more basic: does Kubernetes' own self-healing

822
01:01:39,762 --> 01:01:45,531
correctly and promptly recreate pods that simply vanish, for any reason at all, and do

823
01:01:45,531 --> 01:01:51,300
our topology spread constraints and anti-affinity rules from Lecture 6.1 still hold up correctly under

824
01:01:51,300 --> 01:01:57,069
the specific stress of genuinely losing running pods, not just scaling up gracefully from a

825
01:01:57,069 --> 01:01:58,608
position of comfortable strength.

826
01:01:58,608 --> 01:02:01,300
A more ambitious experiment, on screen: chaos/network-partition.yaml.

827
01:02:01,300 --> 01:02:07,069
This one is doing something considerably more aggressive and more revealing — it identifies which

828
01:02:07,069 --> 01:02:12,838
pods are running in two specific Availability Zones, then applies iptables rules that actively drop

829
01:02:12,838 --> 01:02:18,608
traffic flowing between them, simulating exactly the kind of network-level split that a genuine AZ-level

830
01:02:18,608 --> 01:02:24,377
networking incident could realistically cause, distinct from an entire AZ simply and cleanly disappearing all

831
01:02:24,377 --> 01:02:28,223
at once, the way our Lecture 6.1 cordon test simulated.

832
01:02:28,223 --> 01:02:33,992
Here's why that specific distinction between "AZ disappears cleanly" and "AZ becomes unreachable but is

833
01:02:33,992 --> 01:02:38,992
still technically running" matters more than it might initially sound like it should.

834
01:02:38,992 --> 01:02:44,762
A clean AZ failure is, in a strange way, the easier failure mode to handle

835
01:02:44,762 --> 01:02:50,531
correctly — Kubernetes can clearly and unambiguously see that every node in that zone has

836
01:02:50,531 --> 01:02:55,146
gone unresponsive, and it reschedules everything elsewhere without much genuine ambiguity involved.

837
01:02:55,146 --> 01:03:00,915
A network partition is messier and more dangerous, precisely because it can create what's called

838
01:03:00,915 --> 01:03:06,685
a split-brain scenario: pods in zone A are still very much alive and believe they're

839
01:03:06,685 --> 01:03:12,454
healthy and fully operational, pods in zone B are equally alive and equally convinced of

840
01:03:12,454 --> 01:03:18,223
their own health, but the two groups can no longer communicate with each other or

841
01:03:18,223 --> 01:03:19,377
coordinate at all.

842
01:03:19,377 --> 01:03:25,146
If your system has any component anywhere that assumes it can always freely reach every

843
01:03:25,146 --> 01:03:30,915
other component — some shared, unprotected state, some naive assumption baked into application logic —

844
01:03:30,915 --> 01:03:36,685
a partition is precisely the failure mode most likely to expose that hidden, previously invisible

845
01:03:36,685 --> 01:03:42,454
assumption, in ways a simple, clean pod deletion or a clean AZ cordon never quite

846
01:03:42,454 --> 01:03:43,608
manages to reveal.

847
01:03:43,608 --> 01:03:48,608
Let's make split-brain concrete with a specific example rather than leaving it abstract.

848
01:03:48,608 --> 01:03:54,377
Suppose some piece of your system implements a naive, in-memory rate limiter — a counter

849
01:03:54,377 --> 01:04:00,146
living inside each pod's own local memory, incrementing every time a particular user makes a

850
01:04:00,146 --> 01:04:03,223
request, with no shared, coordinated state across replicas.

851
01:04:03,223 --> 01:04:08,992
Under normal conditions, this is a mild inefficiency at worst, since a user's requests get

852
01:04:08,992 --> 01:04:13,992
spread somewhat randomly across pods, and each pod's local count stays roughly proportional.

853
01:04:13,992 --> 01:04:19,762
During a network partition, it becomes something genuinely worse: zone A's pods and zone B's

854
01:04:19,762 --> 01:04:25,531
pods each keep counting independently, neither aware the other exists at all, and a user

855
01:04:25,531 --> 01:04:31,300
who happens to get bounced between zones could suddenly, briefly exceed a rate limit that

856
01:04:31,300 --> 01:04:37,069
was designed to be a single, unified ceiling, simply because two separate, mutually unaware halves

857
01:04:37,069 --> 01:04:41,300
of your system were each independently, correctly enforcing half of it.

858
01:04:41,300 --> 01:04:42,069
Nothing crashed.

859
01:04:42,069 --> 01:04:43,608
Nothing threw an error.

860
01:04:43,608 --> 01:04:49,377
The system just quietly stopped agreeing with itself about a rule it was supposed to

861
01:04:49,377 --> 01:04:55,146
be enforcing consistently — and a network partition chaos experiment is exactly the kind of

862
01:04:55,146 --> 01:05:00,915
deliberate, controlled test that would actually surface a subtle, easy-to-miss bug like that, long before

863
01:05:00,915 --> 01:05:03,992
a real partition ever gets the chance to.

864
01:05:03,992 --> 01:05:09,762
While chaos experiments run, we watch specific metrics closely, on screen: pod restart counts climbing,

865
01:05:09,762 --> 01:05:13,608
which is generally expected and healthy during a pod-delete experiment.

866
01:05:13,608 --> 01:05:19,377
p95 request latency, tagged specifically with a chaos label so this traffic is cleanly distinguishable

867
01:05:19,377 --> 01:05:21,300
from your regular baseline dashboards.

868
01:05:21,300 --> 01:05:24,377
And error rate, filtered the same deliberate way.

869
01:05:24,377 --> 01:05:30,146
The single most important, genuinely revealing number across the entire experiment isn't really any one

870
01:05:30,146 --> 01:05:35,915
of these snapshotted in isolation — it's the shape of the recovery curve over time.

871
01:05:35,915 --> 01:05:41,685
Does error rate spike briefly and recover cleanly within a handful of seconds, exactly as

872
01:05:41,685 --> 01:05:43,608
your architecture promises it should?

873
01:05:43,608 --> 01:05:49,377
Or does it spike and then linger, stubbornly, well past when the chaos experiment itself

874
01:05:49,377 --> 01:05:53,992
has already ended and every deleted pod has technically already come back?

875
01:05:53,992 --> 01:05:59,762
A slow, straggling recovery curve is often the very first hard, undeniable evidence that something

876
01:05:59,762 --> 01:06:05,531
in your resilience story is more theoretical than actually true in practice — evidence you'd

877
01:06:05,531 --> 01:06:11,300
very strongly rather discover here, on a Tuesday afternoon, running a deliberate, scheduled test, than

878
01:06:11,300 --> 01:06:16,300
during an unplanned real incident with real customers actively affected in real time.

879
01:06:16,300 --> 01:06:22,069
Because chaos experiments genuinely lose most of their value if they only ever get run

880
01:06:22,069 --> 01:06:27,838
manually, once, right after this lecture, and then quietly forgotten about for the next six

881
01:06:27,838 --> 01:06:30,531
months, we schedule them to run automatically.

882
01:06:30,531 --> 01:06:36,300
On screen: .github/workflows/chaos-test.yml, triggered every Sunday at 10 AM — a deliberately chosen off-peak window,

883
01:06:36,300 --> 01:06:42,069
minimizing genuine customer impact from a test that, however controlled and well-intentioned, is still deliberately

884
01:06:42,069 --> 01:06:45,146
breaking things on purpose in a production-adjacent environment.

885
01:06:45,146 --> 01:06:50,915
The workflow applies the pod-delete experiment, waits for it to fully complete, and then explicitly

886
01:06:50,915 --> 01:06:56,685
asserts that the API deployment genuinely returns to a ready state within a two-minute timeout.

887
01:06:56,685 --> 01:07:02,454
If it doesn't — if recovery takes meaningfully longer than that specific, chosen threshold, or

888
01:07:02,454 --> 01:07:08,223
simply never happens cleanly at all — the workflow itself fails loudly, turning "resilience quietly

889
01:07:08,223 --> 01:07:13,992
degraded at some point without anyone noticing" into "a CI job is now red and

890
01:07:13,992 --> 01:07:19,762
actively demanding someone's attention," which is a dramatically better, faster, and cheaper way to find

891
01:07:19,762 --> 01:07:20,462
out.

892
01:07:20,462 --> 01:07:26,231
Now, an honest note before your checkpoint, and one worth taking seriously rather than glossing

893
01:07:26,231 --> 01:07:32,000
over: chaos engineering carries real, genuine risk, and it deserves to be treated with real

894
01:07:32,000 --> 01:07:33,923
operational respect, not casual enthusiasm.

895
01:07:33,923 --> 01:07:39,692
Running these exact experiments against an actual, unmodified production environment, especially early on before you've

896
01:07:39,692 --> 01:07:45,462
built real confidence in your resilience story, can itself directly cause the very outage you're

897
01:07:45,462 --> 01:07:48,538
trying to responsibly guard against and rule out.

898
01:07:48,538 --> 01:07:54,308
Most mature teams start these experiments in staging environments that closely mirror production, then graduate

899
01:07:54,308 --> 01:08:00,077
carefully to production itself only once they've built substantial, demonstrated confidence, and even then, only

900
01:08:00,077 --> 01:08:05,846
during clearly defined low-traffic windows, with a human actively watching dashboards in real time and

901
01:08:05,846 --> 01:08:11,615
a very clear, well-rehearsed plan for immediately aborting the experiment the moment something looks meaningfully

902
01:08:11,615 --> 01:08:12,769
worse than expected.

903
01:08:12,769 --> 01:08:13,923
Here's your checkpoint.

904
01:08:13,923 --> 01:08:19,692
Run the pod-delete experiment against a non-production environment first, and confirm HPA and Kubernetes' own

905
01:08:19,692 --> 01:08:23,154
native self-healing correctly and promptly recreate the deleted pods.

906
01:08:23,154 --> 01:08:28,923
Then run the network partition experiment, and specifically verify that once it ends, cross-zone traffic

907
01:08:28,923 --> 01:08:34,692
genuinely, fully resumes — don't just assume it does because the experiment's own script reported

908
01:08:34,692 --> 01:08:35,846
a clean exit.

909
01:08:35,846 --> 01:08:41,615
Document the recovery time for each experiment individually, and treat any recovery that takes meaningfully

910
01:08:41,615 --> 01:08:47,385
longer than you expected, or genuinely surprises you in any way, not as a chaos-engineering

911
01:08:47,385 --> 01:08:53,154
failure, but as exactly the valuable, load-bearing signal this entire practice exists to surface in

912
01:08:53,154 --> 01:08:54,308
the first place.

913
01:08:54,308 --> 01:09:00,077
Run both experiments against RiskOracle too, not only Financial RAG, and pay attention to something

914
01:09:00,077 --> 01:09:05,846
specific: does a mid-pipeline risk calculation that gets interrupted by a deleted pod ever silently

915
01:09:05,846 --> 01:09:10,462
disappear, or does it correctly resume from where AOF persistence left off?

916
01:09:10,462 --> 01:09:16,231
That's precisely the scenario our earlier Redis checkpoint was built to protect against, and a

917
01:09:16,231 --> 01:09:22,000
chaos experiment is what actually proves that protection holds under a real, unscripted interruption, rather

918
01:09:22,000 --> 01:09:26,231
than the tidy, deliberate test we ran earlier in this lecture.

919
01:09:26,231 --> 01:09:32,000
Here's a phrase worth tattooing somewhere visible on every infrastructure team's shared wiki: a backup

920
01:09:32,000 --> 01:09:35,462
you have never actually restored is not a backup.

921
01:09:35,462 --> 01:09:40,077
It's a hope, dressed up convincingly in the language of engineering rigor.

922
01:09:40,077 --> 01:09:45,846
And this isn't a hypothetical, abstract concern — the specific, recurring failure pattern shows up

923
01:09:45,846 --> 01:09:51,615
constantly in real post-incident write-ups, across companies who otherwise had genuinely competent infrastructure teams: automated

924
01:09:51,615 --> 01:09:57,385
snapshots had been running flawlessly, without a single reported failure, for well over a year.

925
01:09:57,385 --> 01:10:03,154
Nobody had ever actually tried using one to restore anything, under any real or simulated

926
01:10:03,154 --> 01:10:05,077
pressure, in all that time.

927
01:10:05,077 --> 01:10:10,846
Then a real incident happens, someone finally reaches for the very first real restore in

928
01:10:10,846 --> 01:10:16,615
the system's entire history — and discovers, at the worst possible moment, that the backup

929
01:10:16,615 --> 01:10:22,385
format had silently changed months ago after some unrelated engine upgrade, or that required credentials

930
01:10:22,385 --> 01:10:28,154
had quietly expired, or that the restore process itself, which had genuinely never once been

931
01:10:28,154 --> 01:10:33,923
executed end to end, simply doesn't work the way absolutely everyone had confidently assumed it

932
01:10:33,923 --> 01:10:34,623
did.

933
01:10:34,623 --> 01:10:40,392
SOC 2 requires you to actually test your restore process, on a regular cadence, and

934
01:10:40,392 --> 01:10:46,162
this lecture builds exactly that discipline directly into your infrastructure, rather than leaving it as

935
01:10:46,162 --> 01:10:51,931
a manual task that's uncomfortably easy for a busy team to quietly defer, quarter after

936
01:10:51,931 --> 01:10:52,700
quarter, indefinitely.

937
01:10:52,700 --> 01:10:58,469
On screen: k8s/backup-restore-test.yaml, a CronJob scheduled for 2 AM on the first day of every

938
01:10:58,469 --> 01:11:04,238
third month — a genuine, real quarterly cadence, not an aspirational one that exists only

939
01:11:04,238 --> 01:11:10,008
in a policy document somewhere and never actually in a crontab anyone can point to.

940
01:11:10,008 --> 01:11:15,777
Let's walk through exactly what it does, step by careful step, because the specific sequence

941
01:11:15,777 --> 01:11:19,623
itself is the actual evidence your auditor cares about seeing.

942
01:11:19,623 --> 01:11:25,392
First, it creates a fully isolated test namespace, deliberately separated from anything touching production, so

943
01:11:25,392 --> 01:11:31,162
that whatever happens during this restore test genuinely cannot bleed into or disrupt real customer

944
01:11:31,162 --> 01:11:32,700
traffic in any way.

945
01:11:32,700 --> 01:11:38,469
Second, it queries AWS directly for your most recent automated RDS snapshot — deliberately not

946
01:11:38,469 --> 01:11:44,238
a snapshot cherry-picked in advance for being unusually clean or convenient, but whatever real snapshot

947
01:11:44,238 --> 01:11:50,008
your actual, ordinary backup schedule most recently produced on its own, because testing a hand-picked

948
01:11:50,008 --> 01:11:54,623
best-case snapshot would quietly defeat the entire honest point of the exercise.

949
01:11:54,623 --> 01:12:00,392
Third, it restores that snapshot into a genuinely new, temporary RDS instance — importantly, using

950
01:12:00,392 --> 01:12:06,162
a smaller, cheaper instance class than production, because for the specific purpose of validating that

951
01:12:06,162 --> 01:12:11,931
a restore mechanically works and the data inside it is intact, you don't need production-grade

952
01:12:11,931 --> 01:12:17,700
compute sitting behind it, you just need the actual data itself to be genuinely, verifiably

953
01:12:17,700 --> 01:12:18,854
present and correct.

954
01:12:18,854 --> 01:12:24,623
Fourth — and this is the step that meaningfully separates a real, honest test from

955
01:12:24,623 --> 01:12:30,392
mere theater — it doesn't stop at "the restore command completed without throwing an error."

956
01:12:30,392 --> 01:12:36,162
It actively queries the freshly restored database and counts real rows from the last thirty

957
01:12:36,162 --> 01:12:41,931
days, confirming actual, meaningful data genuinely exists inside the restored instance, not just that AWS

958
01:12:41,931 --> 01:12:44,238
reported a technically successful restore operation.

959
01:12:44,238 --> 01:12:50,008
Fifth, it cleans up completely — deleting the temporary test instance and tearing down the

960
01:12:50,008 --> 01:12:55,777
isolated namespace, ensuring this quarterly exercise doesn't quietly leave behind a permanent, ever-growing pile of

961
01:12:55,777 --> 01:13:00,008
forgotten test infrastructure that someone stumbles across, confused, eight months later.

962
01:13:00,008 --> 01:13:05,777
And sixth, it generates a clean, dated, human-readable report and uploads it to S3, becoming

963
01:13:05,777 --> 01:13:11,546
a permanent piece of evidence your team can hand directly to an auditor without needing

964
01:13:11,546 --> 01:13:14,623
to reconstruct or explain anything after the fact.

965
01:13:14,623 --> 01:13:19,238
On screen, an example of exactly what that generated report looks like.

966
01:13:19,238 --> 01:13:22,315
Total restore duration: twelve minutes and thirty-four seconds.

967
01:13:22,315 --> 01:13:28,085
Data validation: forty-five thousand two hundred and thirty-one rows successfully verified as present and intact.

968
01:13:28,085 --> 01:13:33,854
And critically, a specific, deliberate comparison against your stated Recovery Time Objective — your RTO,

969
01:13:33,854 --> 01:13:39,623
the maximum acceptable time your organization has formally committed to for restoring from backup during

970
01:13:39,623 --> 01:13:40,777
a genuine incident.

971
01:13:40,777 --> 01:13:46,546
If your stated RTO is four hours and your test genuinely completes in twelve minutes,

972
01:13:46,546 --> 01:13:52,315
that's not just a reassuring, feel-good number — it's concrete, dated, reproducible evidence that your

973
01:13:52,315 --> 01:13:58,085
recovery commitment is realistic and, more importantly, that it's demonstrably true right now, this quarter,

974
01:13:58,085 --> 01:14:03,854
not merely true in theory or true the one time someone happened to test it

975
01:14:03,854 --> 01:14:05,008
two years ago.

976
01:14:05,008 --> 01:14:10,777
Worth being honest about a real limitation here too, because a good auditor will eventually

977
01:14:10,777 --> 01:14:16,546
probe at it directly: this specific test validates RDS restoration thoroughly and well, but a

978
01:14:16,546 --> 01:14:22,315
genuinely complete disaster recovery story for a Kubernetes-based system also needs to validate restoring your

979
01:14:22,315 --> 01:14:28,085
actual cluster state — deployments, services, configmaps, and the rest — typically using a tool

980
01:14:28,085 --> 01:14:30,392
purpose-built for that job, like Velero.

981
01:14:30,392 --> 01:14:36,162
The report template shown here does include placeholder sections for exactly that, and a genuinely

982
01:14:36,162 --> 01:14:41,931
mature restore-testing practice validates both halves of the story together, side by side, rather than

983
01:14:41,931 --> 01:14:47,700
confidently, thoroughly testing the database half alone while quietly, silently leaving the Kubernetes-state half as

984
01:14:47,700 --> 01:14:48,854
an untested assumption.

985
01:14:48,854 --> 01:14:54,623
Worth also being honest about what happens when a restore test doesn't go well, because

986
01:14:54,623 --> 01:15:00,392
the checkpoint's value depends entirely on treating a bad result as useful information rather than

987
01:15:00,392 --> 01:15:01,931
something to quietly bury.

988
01:15:01,931 --> 01:15:07,700
Suppose your stated RTO is four hours, and your first real quarterly test comes back

989
01:15:07,700 --> 01:15:13,469
at three hours and fifty minutes — technically passing, but only just, with almost no

990
01:15:13,469 --> 01:15:14,623
margin at all.

991
01:15:14,623 --> 01:15:20,008
That's not a green checkmark to file away and forget about until next quarter.

992
01:15:20,008 --> 01:15:25,777
That's a specific, concrete warning that your current process is running uncomfortably close to its

993
01:15:25,777 --> 01:15:31,546
own stated limit, and the very next incident that happens to be slightly larger than

994
01:15:31,546 --> 01:15:37,315
this test, or slightly less lucky in timing, could easily push you over a commitment

995
01:15:37,315 --> 01:15:40,777
you've made publicly, possibly contractually, to your own customers.

996
01:15:40,777 --> 01:15:46,546
A restore test that barely passes deserves exactly the same follow-up attention as one that

997
01:15:46,546 --> 01:15:52,315
fails outright — the only difference is how much runway you still have before it

998
01:15:52,315 --> 01:15:54,238
becomes a real, live problem.

999
01:15:54,238 --> 01:15:55,392
Here's your checkpoint.

1000
01:15:55,392 --> 01:16:01,162
Run this restore test manually, once, right now, rather than waiting for its first scheduled

1001
01:16:01,162 --> 01:16:03,854
quarterly trigger to happen on its own.

1002
01:16:03,854 --> 01:16:09,623
Confirm you can genuinely restore from your most recent real snapshot and that the resulting

1003
01:16:09,623 --> 01:16:10,777
data validates correctly.

1004
01:16:10,777 --> 01:16:16,546
Document your actual restore duration, and compare it honestly against whatever RTO your organization has

1005
01:16:16,546 --> 01:16:22,315
stated, or would state, in its own compliance documentation — because a gap between the

1006
01:16:22,315 --> 01:16:28,085
two isn't just an awkward test result to quietly file away, it's exactly the kind

1007
01:16:28,085 --> 01:16:33,854
of concrete, specific finding you'd want to surface and fix now, deliberately, on your own

1008
01:16:33,854 --> 01:16:39,623
terms and your own schedule, rather than discovering it for the very first time during

1009
01:16:39,623 --> 01:16:41,162
a real, unplanned incident.

1010
01:16:41,162 --> 01:16:46,931
We've now built an enormous amount of genuine resilience, and tested a meaningful share of

1011
01:16:46,931 --> 01:16:47,700
it directly.

1012
01:16:47,700 --> 01:16:53,469
But there's a question still sitting there, unanswered, underneath all of it: at any given

1013
01:16:53,469 --> 01:16:59,238
moment, right now, this second, are you actually meeting the specific availability target you've committed

1014
01:16:59,238 --> 01:16:59,938
to?

1015
01:16:59,938 --> 01:17:05,708
Not "probably," not "it feels pretty solid lately" — a precise, current number you could

1016
01:17:05,708 --> 01:17:09,554
show an auditor, or your own leadership team, without hesitation.

1017
01:17:09,554 --> 01:17:15,323
Here's a mental model that reframes the entire idea of an SLO in a genuinely

1018
01:17:15,323 --> 01:17:21,092
useful way, one that fits neatly with everything we've built and tested throughout this phase:

1019
01:17:21,092 --> 01:17:26,862
think of your 99.9 percent availability target not as a strict pass-fail grade you either

1020
01:17:26,862 --> 01:17:31,862
meet or catastrophically fail, but as a monthly spending budget for allowed downtime.

1021
01:17:31,862 --> 01:17:37,631
At 99.9 percent, you're implicitly allowed roughly forty-three minutes of downtime across an entire month

1022
01:17:37,631 --> 01:17:39,938
before you've fully exhausted that budget.

1023
01:17:39,938 --> 01:17:45,708
Every single minute your system is unavailable, for any reason at all, spends directly against

1024
01:17:45,708 --> 01:17:51,477
that finite monthly allowance — and the moment you view it this way, question shifts

1025
01:17:51,477 --> 01:17:57,246
from a somewhat unhelpful, binary "are we compliant or not" to a genuinely more useful,

1026
01:17:57,246 --> 01:18:03,015
more actionable "how much of our allowed error budget do we currently have left, and

1027
01:18:03,015 --> 01:18:08,785
are we spending it faster or slower than a sustainable pace this month." Let's build

1028
01:18:08,785 --> 01:18:12,246
the queries that actually calculate this in real time.

1029
01:18:12,246 --> 01:18:15,323
On screen: a small set of PromQL queries.

1030
01:18:15,323 --> 01:18:21,092
The first computes your current five-minute success rate — the sum of successful queries divided

1031
01:18:21,092 --> 01:18:25,323
by the sum of total queries, expressed as a clean percentage.

1032
01:18:25,323 --> 01:18:31,092
The second computes the same underlying ratio, but rolled up over a full thirty-day window

1033
01:18:31,092 --> 01:18:36,862
instead of a five-minute one — your actual, honest, rolling availability figure, the specific number

1034
01:18:36,862 --> 01:18:40,323
that genuinely maps directly onto your stated SLO commitment.

1035
01:18:40,323 --> 01:18:46,092
The third is the genuinely interesting one: error budget remaining, expressed specifically as a percentage

1036
01:18:46,092 --> 01:18:51,862
of your total allowed budget for the entire month, rather than as a raw duration

1037
01:18:51,862 --> 01:18:57,631
— this framing tends to communicate urgency far more effectively to a non-engineering audience than

1038
01:18:57,631 --> 01:19:01,092
a raw number of minutes ever quite manages to.

1039
01:19:01,092 --> 01:19:06,862
Eighty percent of your monthly error budget already spent, with two full weeks still remaining

1040
01:19:06,862 --> 01:19:12,631
in the month, is an urgent, actionable signal demanding real attention, in a way that

1041
01:19:12,631 --> 01:19:18,400
"we've had about thirty-four minutes of downtime so far this month" alone simply doesn't communicate

1042
01:19:18,400 --> 01:19:23,015
nearly as clearly or as immediately to someone without deep infrastructure context.

1043
01:19:23,015 --> 01:19:28,785
We surface all of this in a Grafana dashboard, on screen: a JSON definition with

1044
01:19:28,785 --> 01:19:34,554
panels for current availability, rolling thirty-day availability, the error budget gauge itself, estimated total downtime

1045
01:19:34,554 --> 01:19:40,323
for the month expressed in plain seconds, a full historical trend graph, and a breakdown

1046
01:19:40,323 --> 01:19:46,092
table showing availability specifically by individual component — because "the whole system is at 99.95

1047
01:19:46,092 --> 01:19:51,862
percent" can quietly hide one specific component sitting at a genuinely concerning 97 percent the

1048
01:19:51,862 --> 01:19:57,631
entire time, comfortably averaged out and hidden by several other components running essentially flawlessly right

1049
01:19:57,631 --> 01:19:58,400
alongside it.

1050
01:19:58,400 --> 01:20:04,169
Beyond simply displaying the number, we alert on how fast you're consuming it — on

1051
01:20:04,169 --> 01:20:04,938
screen: prometheus/slo-alerts.yaml.

1052
01:20:04,938 --> 01:20:10,708
SLOBurnRateHigh watches specifically for periods where your current, real-time error rate is consuming your monthly

1053
01:20:10,708 --> 01:20:16,477
budget faster than a sustainable pace — the specific insight here being genuinely important and

1054
01:20:16,477 --> 01:20:22,246
easy to miss: a short, five-minute burn rate spiking dramatically above sustainable pace is a

1055
01:20:22,246 --> 01:20:28,015
very different, more urgent situation than a slow, gradual accumulation of many small errors spread

1056
01:20:28,015 --> 01:20:33,785
evenly across an entire month, even if the two scenarios could, in principle, eventually add

1057
01:20:33,785 --> 01:20:37,631
up to the exact same total downtime by month's end.

1058
01:20:37,631 --> 01:20:43,400
ErrorBudgetCritical fires specifically once you've already consumed more than 80 percent of your entire monthly

1059
01:20:43,400 --> 01:20:49,169
allowance, regardless of how quickly or slowly you got there — a clear, unambiguous signal

1060
01:20:49,169 --> 01:20:54,938
that extreme caution, and probably a genuine freeze on risky, non-essential deployments, is now seriously

1061
01:20:54,938 --> 01:20:58,400
warranted for whatever time remains in the current month.

1062
01:20:58,400 --> 01:21:01,477
Here's your final checkpoint for this entire phase.

1063
01:21:01,477 --> 01:21:07,246
Build the availability dashboard using these exact queries, and confirm the numbers it reports genuinely

1064
01:21:07,246 --> 01:21:13,015
match your own manual, independent calculation, at least approximately — don't simply trust a dashboard

1065
01:21:13,015 --> 01:21:15,323
you've never once cross-checked by hand.

1066
01:21:15,323 --> 01:21:21,092
Set up the burn-rate and error-budget alerts, and if you have any way to safely

1067
01:21:21,092 --> 01:21:26,862
do so in a non-production environment, deliberately simulate a failure and confirm the alerts genuinely

1068
01:21:26,862 --> 01:21:30,708
fire as expected, rather than assuming quietly that they will.

1069
01:21:30,708 --> 01:21:36,477
And then, finally, generate one real, complete monthly availability report — the availability-specific sibling of

1070
01:21:36,477 --> 01:21:42,246
the audit report we built back in an earlier phase — because a dashboard that

1071
01:21:42,246 --> 01:21:48,015
only you personally ever look at is only ever going to be as reliable, over

1072
01:21:48,015 --> 01:21:52,631
the long run, as your own personal memory of checking it consistently.

1073
01:21:52,631 --> 01:21:58,400
A monthly report that lands in someone else's inbox automatically, whether you remembered to look

1074
01:21:58,400 --> 01:22:01,862
or not, is genuinely, structurally more durable than that.

1075
01:22:01,862 --> 01:22:07,631
Before we wrap up this phase, let's give it the same honest, balanced treatment we

1076
01:22:07,631 --> 01:22:13,400
owe every serious engineering decision — because everything we've built across these eight lectures comes

1077
01:22:13,400 --> 01:22:19,169
with real costs, and a good auditor, and frankly a good engineer, should want to

1078
01:22:19,169 --> 01:22:22,246
understand those costs clearly, not just the benefits.

1079
01:22:22,246 --> 01:22:28,015
Multi-AZ RDS roughly doubles your database compute cost, since you're continuously running a full standby

1080
01:22:28,015 --> 01:22:31,477
instance that, on most days, does nothing but wait.

1081
01:22:31,477 --> 01:22:34,938
Read replicas add further cost on top of that.

1082
01:22:34,938 --> 01:22:40,708
For an early-stage company with genuinely low traffic, that can reasonably feel like a significant

1083
01:22:40,708 --> 01:22:46,092
expense to take on before it's strictly necessary — and honestly, sometimes it is.

1084
01:22:46,092 --> 01:22:51,862
A thoughtful case exists for running single-AZ in a lower environment like staging, with an

1085
01:22:51,862 --> 01:22:57,631
explicitly documented, higher risk tolerance, reserving full Multi-AZ specifically for whatever system directly touches customer-facing

1086
01:22:57,631 --> 01:22:58,785
data or revenue.

1087
01:22:58,785 --> 01:23:04,554
SOC 2 doesn't require maximum resilience everywhere, uniformly — it requires resilience that's honestly proportionate

1088
01:23:04,554 --> 01:23:08,015
to your stated risk tolerance, and documented as such.

1089
01:23:08,015 --> 01:23:11,092
The autoscaling layer adds genuine operational complexity too.

1090
01:23:11,092 --> 01:23:16,862
A pod that mysteriously won't schedule now potentially involves reasoning through HPA's decision, Karpenter's decision,

1091
01:23:16,862 --> 01:23:19,169
and node capacity all at once.

1092
01:23:19,169 --> 01:23:24,938
For genuinely predictable, steady traffic, a fixed replica count can honestly be simpler and cheaper

1093
01:23:24,938 --> 01:23:29,554
than tuning autoscaling correctly and keeping it tuned as the system evolves.

1094
01:23:29,554 --> 01:23:32,246
Chaos engineering deserves its own honest caveat.

1095
01:23:32,246 --> 01:23:38,015
Done carelessly, without real experience and genuine institutional buy-in, it can directly cause the exact

1096
01:23:38,015 --> 01:23:40,708
customer-facing outages it was meant to prevent.

1097
01:23:40,708 --> 01:23:46,477
Plenty of mature engineering organizations run chaos experiments exclusively in staging, indefinitely, and that's a

1098
01:23:46,477 --> 01:23:52,246
completely legitimate choice — as long as staging genuinely, faithfully represents production's real failure modes,

1099
01:23:52,246 --> 01:23:56,477
rather than being a smaller toy version wearing the same name.

1100
01:23:56,477 --> 01:24:01,092
And here's the broadest point of honest balance to leave you with.

1101
01:24:01,092 --> 01:24:04,938
Ninety-nine point nine percent availability is a serious, hard-won target.

1102
01:24:04,938 --> 01:24:10,708
It was never meant to be ninety-nine point ninety-nine, or ninety-nine point nine nine nine

1103
01:24:10,708 --> 01:24:16,477
— each additional nine costs disproportionately more than the one before it, for steadily diminishing

1104
01:24:16,477 --> 01:24:18,785
real value to your actual customers.

1105
01:24:18,785 --> 01:24:24,554
Ask honestly whether your specific product needs five nines, or whether three nines, done rigorously

1106
01:24:24,554 --> 01:24:30,323
and genuinely tested, actually serves your customers and your business better than chasing an extra

1107
01:24:30,323 --> 01:24:32,246
nine you can't really justify.

1108
01:24:32,246 --> 01:24:38,015
The honest goal was never build the most resilient system technically possible, at any cost.

1109
01:24:38,015 --> 01:24:43,785
It was build a system whose stated target is real, tested, and proportionate to what

1110
01:24:43,785 --> 01:24:45,323
your customers actually need.

1111
01:24:45,323 --> 01:24:47,631
Let's bring the whole phase together.

1112
01:24:47,631 --> 01:24:53,400
We started with a hospital that made a real, binding promise never to go dark,

1113
01:24:53,400 --> 01:24:59,169
and every technique here was one deliberate piece of that promise, engineered into working infrastructure

1114
01:24:59,169 --> 01:25:02,631
rather than left as a comforting sentence nobody tests.

1115
01:25:02,631 --> 01:25:08,400
Multi-AZ RDS and topology spread constraints eliminated the single-AZ blind spot, across both financial-rag and

1116
01:25:08,400 --> 01:25:09,100
RiskOracle.

1117
01:25:09,100 --> 01:25:13,715
Redis AOF persistence closed a narrower, subtler gap for both systems' caches.

1118
01:25:13,715 --> 01:25:19,485
HPA and Karpenter gave you real elasticity, tuned honestly to each system's own real traffic

1119
01:25:19,485 --> 01:25:20,185
pattern.

1120
01:25:20,185 --> 01:25:25,569
PodDisruptionBudgets made sure your own team's maintenance work could never accidentally become the outage.

1121
01:25:25,569 --> 01:25:30,569
Read replicas kept your primaries healthy under real read load, RiskOracle's dashboards especially.

1122
01:25:30,569 --> 01:25:36,338
Chaos engineering turned every claim in this phase into something you personally watched hold up

1123
01:25:36,338 --> 01:25:42,108
under real, injected pressure — for both systems, not just the one you happen to

1124
01:25:42,108 --> 01:25:44,031
spend the most time in.

1125
01:25:44,031 --> 01:25:48,262
Quarterly restore testing made sure your backups were genuinely usable insurance.

1126
01:25:48,262 --> 01:25:54,031
And SLO monitoring, with real error-budget tracking, turned we think we're pretty reliable into a

1127
01:25:54,031 --> 01:25:55,185
precise, defensible number.

1128
01:25:55,185 --> 01:26:00,569
Here's what's concretely true about your systems now, in language your auditor will recognize.

1129
01:26:00,569 --> 01:26:04,415
No single Availability Zone failure can take either system down.

1130
01:26:04,415 --> 01:26:09,415
Data survives routine pod restarts without silent loss, in financial-rag's cache and RiskOracle's.

1131
01:26:09,415 --> 01:26:15,185
Both systems demonstrably scale to meet real demand and scale back down again once it

1132
01:26:15,185 --> 01:26:15,885
passes.

1133
01:26:15,885 --> 01:26:20,885
Your own maintenance work can't accidentally violate your stated commitments, for either system.

1134
01:26:20,885 --> 01:26:23,962
Read load no longer threatens either primary's health.

1135
01:26:23,962 --> 01:26:29,731
You have real, dated evidence — not architectural theory — that both systems recover correctly

1136
01:26:29,731 --> 01:26:31,269
from deliberately injected failure.

1137
01:26:31,269 --> 01:26:37,038
Your backups are proven to work, and you can state your current availability and remaining

1138
01:26:37,038 --> 01:26:40,115
error budget precisely, for both systems, without hedging.

1139
01:26:40,115 --> 01:26:45,885
Let's turn that into a self-audit checklist for CC7.1, walked through the way an auditor's

1140
01:26:45,885 --> 01:26:51,654
own working papers are actually structured — and this time, run through it twice, once

1141
01:26:51,654 --> 01:26:57,423
for financial-rag and once for RiskOracle, because a checklist you've only ever completed for one

